{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788384459835,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_064053c390016bLqad9HeG1YiE","messageID":"msg_064053491001n2kJf90cDpQDmL","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","snapshot":"af4fa15ed4996d0bb919f8e70bd0271c9d7c1cdd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384464263,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8932505e3889dce9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: status: accepted\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n6: \n7: Decided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\n8: appears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n9: \n10: The float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\n11: binary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\n12: the repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\n13: JavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\n14: established the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\n15: setting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\n16: have only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n17: \n18: Not decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\n19: agree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n20: `Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\n21: and reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\n22: associativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\n23: but those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n24: [draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).\n\n(End of file - total 24 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","text":"---\nstatus: accepted\n---\n\n# Float is JavaScript's number: an IEEE 754 binary64 double\n\nDecided 2026-08-27, ahead of implementation: no `Float` exists in the checker yet, and `3.14`\nappears only in the overview page's [values list](../overview/vision.md#values).\n\nThe float type is exactly the standard double every JavaScript engine carries -- IEEE 754\nbinary64 -- with no alternative width and no decimal type. The supporting facts are already in\nthe repository: it is the one numeric representation every backend has natively (for\nJavaScript and jq it is the *only* one), and the Int decision's carrying measurements\nestablished the double's 53-bit integer ceiling as the portable envelope, with JavaScript\nsetting it. Picking anything else would mean emulating a second float on the two targets that\nhave only this one, to gain a width nothing asked for.\n\nNot decided here, named so they are not assumed: how float literals print (six backends must\nagree byte for byte, and default float formatting differs across them), what `NaN` and\n`Infinity` mean in a language whose values are JSON-shaped (JSON has no spelling for either),\nand reduction semantics -- draft.md's vectorization sections already lean on `fold` declaring\nassociativity to make reassociation legitimate, and on keeping floating-point contraction off,\nbut those are operation questions, not representation ones. These are tracked as\n[draft.md's Q37](../../plans/questions.md#q37-how-do-floats-print-and-what-are-nan-and-infinity-in-a-json-shaped-value-model).","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":24,"totalLines":24,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md","time":{"start":1788384464254,"end":1788384464262}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the issue. Let me read the ADR 0007, then find the JS backend's Float implementation as the reference, and understand the Lua backend structure.\n\nLet me look at the key files:\n1. docs/adr/0007-float-is-javascripts-double.md\n2. src/float.rs\n3. src/emit_js.rs (JS reference)\n4. src/emit_lua.rs (Lua backend)\n5. The corpus tests and how JS Float was tested\n\nLet me also look at how the JS backend landed (commit 0c29911 for issue-149). Let me check git log for that.\n\nLet me first check the current state of the branch. It says \"behind origin/main by 14 commits\". Let me check what those commits are, since the JS float implementation landed in a different commit that might not be in this branch. Actually the git log shows 0c29911 \"Land issue-149\" is in this branch's history. Let me verify the current src/emit_js.rs has Float support.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_064054bb1001xUKEYs2Turnrtw","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064053491001n2kJf90cDpQDmL"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384464540,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-870a593ce36feb12","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/float.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/float.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! How a Float is spelled as a number literal (ADR 0007).\n2: //!\n3: //! Every backend emits a literal through here rather than its own target's float formatting,\n4: //! which is what lets the corpus's agreement harness compare their output byte for byte once\n5: //! a second backend carries Float (kantord/toylang#149). Rust's `Display` for `f64` is the\n6: //! shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double -- exactly what a literal needs to be\n7: //! exact on every target, and what JS's own `String(number)` produces for the same value.\n8: \n9: /// A finite binary64 as a number literal. Non-finite values have no literal spelling (they are\n10: /// produced by arithmetic, not written), so one arriving here is a bug upstream.\n11: pub fn lit(n: f64) -> String {\n12:     if !n.is_finite() {\n13:         panic!(\"cannot write a non-finite value as a float literal\");\n14:     }\n15:     n.to_string()\n16: }\n\n(End of file - total 16 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! How a Float is spelled as a number literal (ADR 0007).\n//!\n//! Every backend emits a literal through here rather than its own target's float formatting,\n//! which is what lets the corpus's agreement harness compare their output byte for byte once\n//! a second backend carries Float (kantord/toylang#149). Rust's `Display` for `f64` is the\n//! shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double -- exactly what a literal needs to be\n//! exact on every target, and what JS's own `String(number)` produces for the same value.\n\n/// A finite binary64 as a number literal. Non-finite values have no literal spelling (they are\n/// produced by arithmetic, not written), so one arriving here is a bug upstream.\npub fn lit(n: f64) -> String {\n    if !n.is_finite() {\n        panic!(\"cannot write a non-finite value as a float literal\");\n    }\n    n.to_string()\n}","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/float.rs","text":"//! How a Float is spelled as a number literal (ADR 0007).\n//!\n//! Every backend emits a literal through here rather than its own target's float formatting,\n//! which is what lets the corpus's agreement harness compare their output byte for byte once\n//! a second backend carries Float (kantord/toylang#149). Rust's `Display` for `f64` is the\n//! shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double -- exactly what a literal needs to be\n//! exact on every target, and what JS's own `String(number)` produces for the same value.\n\n/// A finite binary64 as a number literal. Non-finite values have no literal spelling (they are\n/// produced by arithmetic, not written), so one arriving here is a bug upstream.\npub fn lit(n: f64) -> String {\n    if !n.is_finite() {\n        panic!(\"cannot write a non-finite value as a float literal\");\n    }\n    n.to_string()\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":16,"totalLines":16,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/float.rs","time":{"start":1788384464535,"end":1788384464538}},"id":"prt_064054d80001X8jz295iGFActp","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064053491001n2kJf90cDpQDmL"}}
