{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384480567,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b1e6b14d5b5d15e0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_js.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_js.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\n2: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n3: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n4: \n5: /// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n6: /// prefixed.\n7: const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n8: const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n9: \n10: const SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n11: function tl_select(src, pred) {\n12:   const out = [];\n13:   for (let i = 0; i < src.length; i++) if (pred(src[i])) out.push(src[i]);\n14:   return out;\n15: }\n16: \";\n17: \n18: const FIELD_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n19: function tl_field(v, k, depth) {\n20:   if (depth === 0) return v[k];\n21:   const out = [];\n22:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) out[i] = tl_field(v[i], k, depth - 1);\n23:   return out;\n24: }\n25: \";\n26: \n27: const OPT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n28: // An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{some: v}` present, \\\"none\\\" absent.\n29: // Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\n30: function tl_at(v, i, depth) {\n31:   if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_at(e, i, depth - 1));\n32:   const n = v.length;\n33:   if (i < 0) i = n + i;\n34:   if (i < 0 || i >= n) return \\\"none\\\";\n35:   return { some: v[i] };\n36: }\n37: \";\n38: \n39: // `Array.prototype.slice` already clamps out-of-range bounds and counts negatives from the\n40: // end, so jq's boundary behaviour is the target's native one; `undefined` is a bound left\n41: // out, which `.slice` reads as the array's own boundary.\n42: const SLICE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n43: function tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth) {\n44:   if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_slice(e, lo, hi, depth - 1));\n45:   return v.slice(lo, hi);\n46: }\n47: \";\n48: \n49: const TAIL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n50: function tl_tail(v) {\n51:   if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n52:   return { some: v.slice(1) };\n53: }\n54: \";\n55: \n56: const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_unwrap(v, depth) {\n57:   if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_unwrap(e, depth - 1));\n58:   if (v === \"none\") { throw new Error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\"); }\n59:   return v.some;\n60: }\n61: \"#;\n62: \n63: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"// `|0` is ToInt32: it wraps to 32 bits and truncates toward zero, and V8 folds it away once it\n64: // knows the value is already a Smi.\n65: function tl_div(a, b) {\n66:   if (b === 0) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n67:   return (a / b) | 0;\n68: }\n69: function tl_rem(a, b) {\n70:   if (b === 0) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n71:   return (a % b) | 0;\n72: }\n73: \"#;\n74: \n75: // An Int64 is a BigInt (kantord/toylang#83): a double is off past 2^53, and BigInt's own `/`\n76: // and `%` already truncate with the dividend's sign. `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the wrap,\n77: // including `MIN / -1` back to `MIN`.\n78: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_div64(a, b) {\n79:   if (b === 0n) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n80:   return BigInt.asIntN(64, a / b);\n81: }\n82: function tl_rem64(a, b) {\n83:   if (b === 0n) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n84:   return BigInt.asIntN(64, a % b);\n85: }\n86: \"#;\n87: \n88: const COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"// Synchronous, because a toylang expression evaluates to completion and node has no\n89: // synchronous line reader built in. Reads in fixed chunks off the real fd rather than\n90: // `readFileSync(0)`, so a line is available to the rest of the program as soon as it arrives\n91: // rather than only once stdin closes.\n92: function tl_collect_lines() {\n93:   const fs = require(\"fs\");\n94:   const out = [];\n95:   let buf = \"\";\n96:   const chunk = Buffer.alloc(65536);\n97:   for (;;) {\n98:     const n = fs.readSync(0, chunk, 0, chunk.length, null);\n99:     if (n === 0) break;\n100:     buf += chunk.toString(\"utf8\", 0, n);\n101:     let i;\n102:     while ((i = buf.indexOf(\"\\n\")) !== -1) {\n103:       out.push(buf.slice(0, i));\n104:       buf = buf.slice(i + 1);\n105:     }\n106:   }\n107:   if (buf.length > 0) out.push(buf);\n108:   return out;\n109: }\n110: \"#;\n111: \n112: const JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n113: function tl_join(v, f) {\n114:   const parts = [];\n115:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n116:   return \\\"[\\\" + parts.join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"]\\\";\n117: }\n118: \";\n119: \n120: // JS string comparison compares UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint order on any\n121: // pair straddling a surrogate pair: a BMP character above U+DFFF (e.g. U+E000) sorts below an\n122: // astral character's lead surrogate even though its codepoint is smaller. Iterating with the\n123: // string iterator (rather than indexing) steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included.\n124: const STR_CMP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n125: function tl_str_cmp(a, b) {\n126:   const ai = a[Symbol.iterator]();\n127:   const bi = b[Symbol.iterator]();\n128:   for (;;) {\n129:     const x = ai.next();\n130:     const y = bi.next();\n131:     if (x.done || y.done) return (x.done ? 0 : 1) - (y.done ? 0 : 1);\n132:     const cx = x.value.codePointAt(0);\n133:     const cy = y.value.codePointAt(0);\n134:     if (cx !== cy) return cx < cy ? -1 : 1;\n135:   }\n136: }\n137: \";\n138: \n139: // A record and a payload-carrying variant are both objects, and JS `===` on two objects is\n140: // identity, so `{a: 1} === {a: 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n141: // Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n142: // two spellings of one record type may reach here with their keys in different orders.\n143: const EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n144: function tl_eq(a, b) {\n145:   if (a === b) return true;\n146:   if (typeof a !== \\\"object\\\" || a === null || typeof b !== \\\"object\\\" || b === null) return false;\n147:   const keys = Object.keys(a);\n148:   if (keys.length !== Object.keys(b).length) return false;\n149:   return keys.every((k) => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && tl_eq(a[k], b[k]));\n150: }\n151: \";\n152: \n153: const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n154: function tl_jsonlines(v, f) {\n155:   const parts = [];\n156:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n157:   return parts.join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\n158: }\n159: \";\n160: \n161: // The string iterator steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included (the same\n162: // iterator STR_CMP_HELPER uses above), so there is no decoding to get right here.\n163: const CHARS_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n164: function tl_chars(s) {\n165:   return Array.from(s, (c) => c.codePointAt(0));\n166: }\n167: \";\n168: \n169: // Int and Int64 are different runtime types here -- Number and BigInt -- so `+` cannot mix\n170: // them and each width needs its own fold. `|0` is the 32-bit wrap (the same ToInt32 ARITH_HELPER\n171: // relies on); `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the 64-bit one ARITH64_HELPER uses.\n172: const SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n173: function tl_sum(v) {\n174:   let acc = 0;\n175:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = (acc + v[i]) | 0;\n176:   return acc;\n177: }\n178: function tl_sum64(v) {\n179:   let acc = 0n;\n180:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = BigInt.asIntN(64, acc + v[i]);\n181:   return acc;\n182: }\n183: \";\n184: \n185: // `>` orders Number and BigInt alike, so one maximum serves both widths.\n186: const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n187: function tl_max(v) {\n188:   if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n189:   let m = v[0];\n190:   for (let i = 1; i < v.length; i++) if (v[i] > m) m = v[i];\n191:   return { some: m };\n192: }\n193: \";\n194: \n195: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n196:     let enums = &program.enums;\n197:     let mut out = String::new();\n198: \n199:     let used = used_helpers(program);\n200:     let join = matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Vec(_))\n201:         || contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)\n202:         || used.jsonlines;\n203:     for (on, text) in [\n204:         (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n205:         (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n206:         (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n207:         (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n208:         (used.slice, SLICE_HELPER),\n209:         (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n210:         (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n211:         (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n212:         (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n213:         (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n214:         (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n215:         (used.str_cmp, STR_CMP_HELPER),\n216:         (used.chars, CHARS_HELPER),\n217:         (used.sum || used.sum64, SUM_HELPER),\n218:         (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n219:         (used.eq, EQ_HELPER),\n220:     ] {\n221:         if on {\n222:             out.push_str(text);\n223:         }\n224:     }\n225:     out.push_str(&printers(program));\n226: \n227:     // Function declarations hoist, so a call to one defined further down resolves without the\n228:     // forward declarations Lua needs. Each backend does what its own target does.\n229:     for f in &program.funcs {\n230:         let param = f.param.as_deref().map(user);\n231:         if tir::has_tail_call(&f.name, &f.body) {\n232:             // The contract: a self-tail-call runs in constant stack. A tail call becomes\n233:             // `param = arg; continue;` in a loop, so 100k-deep self-recursion cannot blow the\n234:             // JS stack the way a real call would (kantord/toylang#141).\n235:             let mut fresh = 0;\n236:             out.push_str(&format!(\"function {}({}) {{\\n\", user(&f.name), param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default()));\n237:             out.push_str(\"  while (true) {\\n\");\n238:             out.push_str(&indent(&indent(&tail_stmts(\n239:                 enums, &f.name, param.as_deref(), &mut fresh, &f.body\n240:             ))));\n241:             out.push_str(\"  }\\n}\\n\");\n242:         } else {\n243:             out.push_str(&format!(\n244:                 \"function {}({}) {{\\n  return {};\\n}}\\n\",\n245:                 user(&f.name),\n246:                 param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n247:                 expr(enums, &f.body)\n248:             ));\n249:         }\n250:     }\n251: \n252:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n253:         out.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n254:         return out;\n255:     }\n256: \n257:     if program.input.is_some() {\n258:         out.push_str(&format!(\n259:             \"const {INPUT} = JSON.parse(require(\\\"fs\\\").readFileSync(0, \\\"utf8\\\"));\\n\"\n260:         ));\n261:     }\n262:     if program.inputs.is_some() {\n263:         out.push_str(&format!(\n264:             \"const {INPUTS} = require(\\\"fs\\\").readFileSync(0, \\\"utf8\\\").split(\\\"\\\\n\\\").filter((l) => l.length > 0).map((l) => JSON.parse(l));\\n\"\n265:         ));\n266:     }\n267: \n268:     let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n269:     // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n270:     if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n271:         out.push_str(&format!(\"console.log({body});\\n\"));\n272:     } else {\n273:         out.push_str(&format!(\n274:             \"console.log({});\\n\",\n275:             show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n276:         ));\n277:     }\n278:     out\n279: }\n280: \n281: /// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop reading one line at a time off the\n282: /// real fd rather than `readFileSync(0)`'s read-everything-first.\n283: ///\n284: /// `process.stdout.write` is synchronous to a file or TTY on POSIX but asynchronous to a pipe --\n285: /// queued rather than issued immediately -- and the very next thing this loop does is a\n286: /// *synchronous* read that blocks the whole event loop, which risks stopping a queued write from\n287: /// ever reaching the pipe before the process blocks again. `tests/streaming.rs`'s `js_streams`\n288: /// checks this directly against a live pipe rather than assuming either way.\n289: fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n290:     let enums = &program.enums;\n291:     let mut out = String::new();\n292:     let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n293:         tir::Source::Inputs => {\n294:             out.push_str(&read_line_helper());\n295:             out.push_str(\"for (;;) {\\n\");\n296:             out.push_str(\"  const t_line_raw = tl_read_line();\\n\");\n297:             out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw === null) break;\\n\");\n298:             out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw.length === 0) continue;\\n\");\n299:             out.push_str(\"  const t_line = JSON.parse(t_line_raw);\\n\");\n300:             let elem = program\n301:                 .inputs\n302:                 .as_ref()\n303:                 .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n304:             (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n305:         }\n306:         // A raw line is already the element, blank ones included: `lines` keeps them.\n307:         tir::Source::Lines => {\n308:             out.push_str(&read_line_helper());\n309:             out.push_str(\"for (;;) {\\n\");\n310:             out.push_str(\"  const t_line_raw = tl_read_line();\\n\");\n311:             out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw === null) break;\\n\");\n312:             (\"t_line_raw\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n313:         }\n314:         // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n315:         // the loop body never run, the same answer `tl_range` gives eagerly.\n316:         tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n317:             out.push_str(&format!(\"const n = {};\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n318:             out.push_str(\"for (let t_i = 0; t_i < n; t_i++) {\\n\");\n319:             (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n320:         }\n321:     };\n322:     for stage in &fusion.stages {\n323:         match stage {\n324:             tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n325:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n326:                 current = expr(enums, body);\n327:                 current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n328:             }\n329:             tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n330:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n331:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"  if (!({})) continue;\\n\", expr(enums, pred)));\n332:                 current = local(*param);\n333:             }\n334:         }\n335:     }\n336:     out.push_str(&format!(\n337:         \"  console.log({});\\n\",\n338:         show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0)\n339:     ));\n340:     out.push_str(\"}\\n\");\n341:     out\n342: }\n343: \n344: /// The read-one-line-at-a-time machinery a stdin-backed fused loop needs. A `range` source reads\n345: /// nothing, so it never emits this.\n346: fn read_line_helper() -> String {\n347:     let mut out = String::new();\n348:     out.push_str(\"let tl_stdin_buf = \\\"\\\";\\n\");\n349:     out.push_str(\"let tl_stdin_eof = false;\\n\");\n350:     out.push_str(\"function tl_read_line() {\\n\");\n351:     out.push_str(\"  const fs = require(\\\"fs\\\");\\n\");\n352:     out.push_str(\"  for (;;) {\\n\");\n353:     out.push_str(\"    const i = tl_stdin_buf.indexOf(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n\");\n354:     out.push_str(\"    if (i !== -1) {\\n\");\n355:     out.push_str(\"      const line = tl_stdin_buf.slice(0, i);\\n\");\n356:     out.push_str(\"      tl_stdin_buf = tl_stdin_buf.slice(i + 1);\\n\");\n357:     out.push_str(\"      return line;\\n\");\n358:     out.push_str(\"    }\\n\");\n359:     out.push_str(\"    if (tl_stdin_eof) {\\n\");\n360:     out.push_str(\"      if (tl_stdin_buf.length === 0) return null;\\n\");\n361:     out.push_str(\"      const line = tl_stdin_buf;\\n\");\n362:     out.push_str(\"      tl_stdin_buf = \\\"\\\";\\n\");\n363:     out.push_str(\"      return line;\\n\");\n364:     out.push_str(\"    }\\n\");\n365:     out.push_str(\"    const chunk = Buffer.alloc(65536);\\n\");\n366:     out.push_str(\"    const n = fs.readSync(0, chunk, 0, chunk.length, null);\\n\");\n367:     out.push_str(\"    if (n === 0) { tl_stdin_eof = true; continue; }\\n\");\n368:     out.push_str(\"    tl_stdin_buf += chunk.toString(\\\"utf8\\\", 0, n);\\n\");\n369:     out.push_str(\"  }\\n\");\n370:     out.push_str(\"}\\n\");\n371:     out\n372: }\n373: \n374: /// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, so a record's keys\n375: /// are known and ordered at compile time, in declaration order. That removes the whole class\n376: /// of disagreement where one backend enumerates keys in insertion order and another sorts\n377: /// them, and it is what a native backend will have to do anyway, having no runtime type\n378: /// information at all.\n379: fn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n380:     match ty {\n381:         Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n382:         // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n383:         // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n384:         Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n385:         Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n386:         Type::Str => format!(\"JSON.stringify({value})\"),\n387:         Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n388:         // String() on a BigInt is the bare digits, no `n` suffix, so Int64 rides the same arm;\n389:         // on a Number it is JS's shortest round-trip spelling, which is exactly how a Float\n390:         // prints (Infinity and NaN come out as their names).\n391:         Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float | Type::Bool => format!(\"String({value})\"),\n392:         Type::Vec(elem) => {\n393:             let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n394:             format!(\n395:                 \"tl_join({value}, ({e}) => {})\",\n396:                 show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n397:             )\n398:         }\n399:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n400:             let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n401:             let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n402:             format!(\n403:                 \"(({v}) => {v} === \\\"none\\\" ? \\\"null\\\" : {})({value})\",\n404:                 show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}.some\"), depth + 1)\n405:             )\n406:         }\n407:         // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n408:         // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n409:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n410:         Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n411:         Type::Record(fields) => {\n412:             // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n413:             // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n414:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n415:                 .iter()\n416:                 .map(|(name, fty)| {\n417:                     let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", js_string(name));\n418:                     let key = js_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n419:                     format!(\"{key} + {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n420:                 })\n421:                 .collect();\n422:             format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" + [{}].join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\", \"))\n423:         }\n424:     }\n425: }\n426: \n427: /// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n428: /// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key object, so the shape is inspected before\n429: /// rendering. Which payload follows which key is still the type's knowledge, as everywhere else.\n430: fn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n431:     let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n432:     let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n433:     let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n434:         variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n435:     if payloads.is_empty() {\n436:         return format!(\"JSON.stringify({value})\");\n437:     }\n438:     let mut body = String::new();\n439:     if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n440:         body.push_str(&format!(\n441:             \"typeof {n} === \\\"string\\\" ? JSON.stringify({n}) : \"\n442:         ));\n443:     }\n444:     for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n445:         let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n446:         let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", js_string(vname));\n447:         let wrapped = format!(\n448:             \"({} + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\",\n449:             js_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n450:             show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n451:         );\n452:         if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n453:             body.push_str(&format!(\"{read} !== undefined ? {wrapped} : \"));\n454:         } else {\n455:             // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n456:             body.push_str(&wrapped);\n457:         }\n458:     }\n459:     format!(\"(({n}) => {body})({value})\")\n460: }\n461: \n462: /// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n463: /// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n464: /// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\n465: fn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n466:     let mut out = String::new();\n467:     for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n468:         out.push_str(&format!(\n469:             \"function {}(v) {{\\n  return {};\\n}}\\n\",\n470:             ty.show_fn(),\n471:             show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n472:         ));\n473:     }\n474:     out\n475: }\n476: \n477: fn contains_vec(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n478:     match ty {\n479:         Type::Vec(_) => true,\n480:         Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| contains_vec(enums, t)),\n481:         // The Vec arm answers before this one can loop: a self-reference is legal only behind\n482:         // a Vec, so a recursive enum's first payload hop is one.\n483:         Type::Enum { .. } => ty::variants(enums, ty)\n484:             .iter()\n485:             .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| contains_vec(enums, p))),\n486:         _ => false,\n487:     }\n488: }\n489: \n490: fn user(name: &str) -> String {\n491:     format!(\"v_{name}\")\n492: }\n493: \n494: fn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n495:     format!(\"t_{id}\")\n496: }\n497: \n498: #[derive(Default)]\n499: struct Helpers {\n500:     select: bool,\n501:     field: bool,\n502:     index: bool,\n503:     slice: bool,\n504:     unwrap: bool,\n505:     arith: bool,\n506:     arith64: bool,\n507:     collect: bool,\n508:     jsonlines: bool,\n509:     tail: bool,\n510:     str_cmp: bool,\n511:     chars: bool,\n512:     sum: bool,\n513:     sum64: bool,\n514:     max: bool,\n515:     eq: bool,\n516: }\n517: \n518: /// Which helper a comparison reaches for, decided by the operator and the operand type rather\n519: /// than by anything in the tree below it: ordering on a Str has to step by codepoint, and\n520: /// equality on a composite has to walk the structure.\n521: fn compare_helpers(op: BinOp, operand: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n522:     used.str_cmp |=\n523:         *operand == Type::Str && matches!(op, BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge);\n524:     used.eq |= operand.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne);\n525: }\n526: \n527: /// Two of the six comparison operators cannot be handed to JS as they are written.\n528: /// `<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=` on native strings compare UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint\n529: /// order across a surrogate pair, so `tl_str_cmp` steps by codepoint instead; equality is\n530: /// unaffected there (the same codepoints give the same units either way). `===` on two objects\n531: /// is identity, so equality on a composite walks the structure through `tl_eq`\n532: /// (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate open question and keeps whatever\n533: /// JS does with it.\n534: fn compare(enums: &Enums, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n535:     if lhs.ty.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n536:         let call = format!(\"tl_eq({}, {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs));\n537:         return match op {\n538:             BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(!{call})\"),\n539:             _ => call,\n540:         };\n541:     }\n542:     if lhs.ty == Type::Str && matches!(op, BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge) {\n543:         return format!(\n544:             \"(tl_str_cmp({}, {}) {} 0)\",\n545:             expr(enums, lhs),\n546:             expr(enums, rhs),\n547:             js_op(op)\n548:         );\n549:     }\n550:     format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), js_op(op), expr(enums, rhs))\n551: }\n552: \n553: /// `+` on two arrays stringifies and joins them, so a Vec reaches for `.concat` instead.\n554: fn concat(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n555:     match ty {\n556:         Type::Vec(_) => format!(\"{}.concat({})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n557:         _ => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n558:     }\n559: }\n560: \n561: fn used_helpers(program: &Program) -> Helpers {\n562:     fn walk(t: &Tir, used: &mut Helpers) {\n563:         match &t.kind {\n564:             Kind::Str(_)\n565:             | Kind::Int(_)\n566:             | Kind::Float(_)\n567:             | Kind::Var(_)\n568:             | Kind::Local(_)\n569:             | Kind::Input\n570:             | Kind::Inputs => {}\n571:             // The source is what reads stdin now, so it is what needs the helper.\n572:             Kind::Lines => used.collect = true,\n573:             Kind::Dsv { .. } => used.collect = true,\n574:             Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, used)),\n575:             Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n576:                 fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, used));\n577:             }\n578:             // A variant's payload and a call's argument are the same shape here -- one optional\n579:             // child, recursed into and nothing else -- so they share the arm.\n580:             Kind::EnumLit { payload: child, .. } | Kind::Call { arg: child, .. } => {\n581:                 if let Some(c) = child {\n582:                     walk(c, used);\n583:                 }\n584:             }\n585:             Kind::Concat(l, r) | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n586:                 walk(l, used);\n587:                 walk(r, used);\n588:             }\n589:             Kind::Compare { op, lhs: l, rhs: r } => {\n590:                 compare_helpers(*op, &l.ty, used);\n591:                 walk(l, used);\n592:                 walk(r, used);\n593:             }\n594:             Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n595:                 walk(value, used);\n596:                 walk(body, used);\n597:             }\n598:             Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n599:                 walk(source, used);\n600:                 walk(body, used);\n601:             }\n602:             Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n603:                 used.select = true;\n604:                 walk(source, used);\n605:                 walk(pred, used);\n606:             }\n607:             Kind::Field { base, .. } => {\n608:                 used.field |= tir::vec_depth(&base.ty) > 0;\n609:                 walk(base, used);\n610:             }\n611:             Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n612:                 used.jsonlines |= *which == Builtin::JsonLines;\n613:                 used.tail |= *which == Builtin::Tail;\n614:                 used.chars |= *which == Builtin::Chars;\n615:                 used.str_cmp |=\n616:                     *which == Builtin::Sort && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Str);\n617:                 used.sum |= *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n618:                 used.sum64 |=\n619:                     *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int64);\n620:                 used.max |= *which == Builtin::Max;\n621:                 walk(arg, used);\n622:             }\n623:             Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n624:                 if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n625:                     used.arith64 = true;\n626:                 } else if t.ty == Type::Int {\n627:                     used.arith = true;\n628:                 }\n629:                 walk(lhs, used);\n630:                 walk(rhs, used);\n631:             }\n632:             Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, used),\n633:             Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n634:                 used.unwrap = true;\n635:                 walk(base, used);\n636:             }\n637:             Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n638:                 used.index = true;\n639:                 walk(base, used);\n640:                 walk(index, used);\n641:             }\n642:             Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n643:                 used.slice = true;\n644:                 walk(base, used);\n645:                 if let Some(s) = start {\n646:                     walk(s, used);\n647:                 }\n648:                 if let Some(e) = end {\n649:                     walk(e, used);\n650:                 }\n651:             }\n652:             Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n653:                 walk(subject, used);\n654:                 for a in arms {\n655:                     if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n656:                         walk(g, used);\n657:                     }\n658:                     walk(&a.body, used);\n659:                 }\n660:             }\n661:         }\n662:     }\n663:     let mut used = Helpers::default();\n664:     program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut used));\n665:     walk(&program.body, &mut used);\n666:     used\n667: }\n668: \n669: /// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged; total chains return the\n670: /// body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm (kantord/toylang#62).\n671: fn arm_return(body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n672:     if partial {\n673:         format!(\"return {{some: {body}}}; \")\n674:     } else {\n675:         format!(\"return {body}; \")\n676:     }\n677: }\n678: \n679: /// `t` as statements in the tail position: `return <expr>;` for a base case, and for a tail\n680: /// call `param = <arg>; continue;` so the loop in the emitted function rewinds instead of\n681: /// growing the JS stack. Lines come out unindented; `indent` places them under the `while`.\n682: fn tail_stmts(\n683:     enums: &Enums,\n684:     name: &str,\n685:     param: Option<&str>,\n686:     fresh: &mut usize,\n687:     t: &Tir,\n688: ) -> String {\n689:     match &t.kind {\n690:         Kind::Call { func, arg } if func == name => {\n691:             let assign = param.map_or_else(String::new, |p| {\n692:                 format!(\n693:                     \"{p} = {};\\n\",\n694:                     arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n695:                 )\n696:             });\n697:             format!(\"{assign}continue;\\n\")\n698:         }\n699:         Kind::Bind {\n700:             local: id,\n701:             value,\n702:             body,\n703:         } => format!(\n704:             \"const {} = {};\\n{}\",\n705:             local(*id),\n706:             expr(enums, value),\n707:             tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, body)\n708:         ),\n709:         Kind::Match {\n710:             subject,\n711:             arms,\n712:             partial,\n713:         } if !partial => {\n714:             // The subject is read into a temp the way `expr`'s IIFE does, but there is no IIFE\n715:             // to contain it here, so the name has to be fresh rather than the fixed `subj`.\n716:             let subj = format!(\"tl_tailsub{}\", *fresh);\n717:             *fresh += 1;\n718:             let mut out = format!(\"const {subj} = {};\\n\", expr(enums, subject));\n719:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n720:                 let mut run = String::new();\n721:                 if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n722:                     let variant = arm\n723:                         .variant\n724:                         .as_ref()\n725:                         .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n726:                     run.push_str(&format!(\n727:                         \"const {} = {subj}[{}];\\n\",\n728:                         local(pid),\n729:                         js_string(variant)\n730:                     ));\n731:                 }\n732:                 run.push_str(&tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, &arm.body));\n733:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n734:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n735:                         Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] !== undefined\", js_string(v)))\n736:                     }\n737:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} === {}\", js_string(v))),\n738:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n739:                     (None, None) => None,\n740:                 };\n741:                 match test {\n742:                     // A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having proved nothing\n743:                     // else can reach it -- the same rule `expr`'s match arm follows.\n744:                     Some(test) if i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n745:                         out.push_str(&format!(\"if ({test}) {{\\n{}}}\\n\", indent(&run)));\n746:                     }\n747:                     _ => out.push_str(&run),\n748:                 }\n749:             }\n750:             out\n751:         }\n752:         // A partial match wraps every arm body, so no arm body is a tail position; the whole\n753:         // match stays an expression, exactly as it would outside tail position.\n754:         Kind::Match { .. } => format!(\"return {};\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n755:         _ => format!(\"return {};\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n756:     }\n757: }\n758: \n759: /// Prepend two spaces to every line. `tail_stmts` returns its lines unindented, and each\n760: /// nesting level (`while` body, an `if` branch) shifts by one indent.\n761: fn indent(s: &str) -> String {\n762:     let mut out = String::new();\n763:     for line in s.trim_end_matches('\\n').split('\\n') {\n764:         out.push_str(\"  \");\n765:         out.push_str(line);\n766:         out.push('\\n');\n767:     }\n768:     out\n769: }\n770: \n771: fn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n772:     match &t.kind {\n773:         Kind::Str(s) => js_string(s),\n774:         Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n),\n775:         Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n),\n776:         Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n777:         Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n778:         Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n779:         Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n780:         // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n781:         // works on the array of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n782:         Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n783:         // Same raw lines as `lines`, each split on the delimiter into one row.\n784:         Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n785:             \"tl_collect_lines().map((l) => l.split({}))\",\n786:             js_string(delim)\n787:         ),\n788:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n789:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n790:                 .iter()\n791:                 .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: {}\", js_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n792:                 .collect();\n793:             // Parenthesised because a brace opening an arrow function's body is a block, so\n794:             // `(e) => {a: 1}` is a labelled statement rather than an object.\n795:             format!(\"({{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n796:         }\n797: \n798:         // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n799:         // variant the single-key object a record already is.\n800:         Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n801:             None => js_string(variant),\n802:             Some(p) => format!(\"({{{}: {}}})\", js_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n803:         },\n804: \n805:         Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n806:             let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n807:             format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n808:         }\n809:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n810:             \"{}({})\",\n811:             user(func),\n812:             arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n813:         ),\n814:         Kind::Concat(l, r) => concat(enums, &t.ty, l, r),\n815:         Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n816:         Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n817:             let op = match op {\n818:                 LogicOp::And => \"&&\",\n819:                 LogicOp::Or => \"||\",\n820:             };\n821:             format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs))\n822:         }\n823:         Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(!{})\", expr(enums, base)),\n824:         Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n825:             Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"String({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n826:             // The one real conversion among the backends: an Int is a number and an Int64 is\n827:             // a BigInt, and BigInt() of a 32-bit integer is always exact.\n828:             Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"BigInt({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n829:             Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n830:             Builtin::Range => {\n831:                 format!(\n832:                     \"Array.from({{ length: Math.max(0, {}) }}, (_, i) => i)\",\n833:                     expr(enums, arg)\n834:                 )\n835:             }\n836:             Builtin::JsonLines => {\n837:                 let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n838:                 let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n839:                 format!(\n840:                     \"tl_jsonlines({}, ({e}) => {})\",\n841:                     expr(enums, arg),\n842:                     show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n843:                 )\n844:             }\n845:             // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n846:             Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n847:             Builtin::Length => format!(\"{}.length\", expr(enums, arg)),\n848:             Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n849:             Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"{}.flat()\", expr(enums, arg)),\n850:             // `Array.prototype.sort`'s default comparator stringifies, which is wrong for\n851:             // numbers; `tl_str_cmp` already returns the -1/0/1 a comparator wants, so it can be\n852:             // passed straight through for Str.\n853:             Builtin::Sort => {\n854:                 let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec\");\n855:                 if *elem == Type::Str {\n856:                     format!(\"[...{}].sort(tl_str_cmp)\", expr(enums, arg))\n857:                 } else {\n858:                     // `a - b` would coerce the comparator's result with ToNumber, which\n859:                     // throws on BigInt -- and Int64 is BigInt here. The three-way compare\n860:                     // returns plain -1/0/1 for Number and BigInt alike.\n861:                     format!(\n862:                         \"[...{}].sort((a, b) => a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0)\",\n863:                         expr(enums, arg)\n864:                     )\n865:                 }\n866:             }\n867:             // `.reverse()` mutates in place, so the spread copy is what keeps this an ordinary\n868:             // expression rather than a statement with a visible side effect on `arg`.\n869:             Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"[...{}].reverse()\", expr(enums, arg)),\n870:             // One fold per width, since the element type picks the runtime integer type.\n871:             Builtin::Sum => {\n872:                 if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n873:                     format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n874:                 } else {\n875:                     format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n876:                 }\n877:             }\n878:             Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n879:             // The names come from the checked type, not the object value, so `arg` is evaluated\n880:             // only for whatever else it does (a division inside it must still throw) and its\n881:             // value discarded with the comma operator.\n882:             Builtin::Fields => {\n883:                 let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n884:                     unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n885:                 };\n886:                 let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| js_string(n)).collect();\n887:                 format!(\"({}, [{}])\", expr(enums, arg), names.join(\", \"))\n888:             }\n889:         },\n890:         Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(enums, *op, lhs, rhs),\n891:         Kind::Bind {\n892:             local: id,\n893:             value,\n894:             body,\n895:         } => {\n896:             format!(\n897:                 \"(({}) => {})({})\",\n898:                 local(*id),\n899:                 expr(enums, body),\n900:                 expr(enums, value)\n901:             )\n902:         }\n903:         // Array.prototype.map is exactly this, so no helper is needed.\n904:         Kind::Map {\n905:             source,\n906:             param,\n907:             body,\n908:         } => {\n909:             format!(\n910:                 \"{}.map(({}) => {})\",\n911:                 expr(enums, source),\n912:                 local(*param),\n913:                 expr(enums, body)\n914:             )\n915:         }\n916:         Kind::Select {\n917:             source,\n918:             param,\n919:             pred,\n920:         } => {\n921:             format!(\n922:                 \"tl_select({}, ({}) => {})\",\n923:                 expr(enums, source),\n924:                 local(*param),\n925:                 expr(enums, pred)\n926:             )\n927:         }\n928:         // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the tagged shape (`\"none\"` or `{some: v}`)\n929:         // is generic enough that this is the ordinary key-presence test every Match arm over\n930:         // an Opt subject would already use, just rebuilding the payload instead of a body.\n931:         Kind::OptMap {\n932:             source,\n933:             param,\n934:             body,\n935:         } => {\n936:             format!(\n937:                 \"(__opt => __opt === \\\"none\\\" ? \\\"none\\\" : (({}) => ({{some: {}}}))(__opt.some))({})\",\n938:                 local(*param),\n939:                 expr(enums, body),\n940:                 expr(enums, source)\n941:             )\n942:         }\n943:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n944:             format!(\n945:                 \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n946:                 expr(enums, base),\n947:                 tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n948:             )\n949:         }\n950:         Kind::Index {\n951:             base, index, depth, ..\n952:         } => {\n953:             format!(\n954:                 \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n955:                 expr(enums, base),\n956:                 expr(enums, index),\n957:                 depth\n958:             )\n959:         }\n960:         Kind::Slice {\n961:             base, start, end, depth,\n962:         } => {\n963:             let lo = match start {\n964:                 Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n965:                 None => \"undefined\".to_string(),\n966:             };\n967:             let hi = match end {\n968:                 Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n969:                 None => \"undefined\".to_string(),\n970:             };\n971:             format!(\n972:                 \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n973:                 expr(enums, base),\n974:                 lo,\n975:                 hi,\n976:                 depth\n977:             )\n978:         }\n979:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n980:             let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n981:             if depth == 0 {\n982:                 format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), js_string(name))\n983:             } else {\n984:                 format!(\n985:                     \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n986:                     expr(enums, base),\n987:                     js_string(name),\n988:                     depth\n989:                 )\n990:             }\n991:         }\n992:         // A chain of tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): string\n993:         // equality for a unit variant, key presence for a payload variant, the guard's own\n994:         // Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n995:         // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tags every present arm and falls\n996:         // through to the absent Opt.\n997:         Kind::Match {\n998:             subject,\n999:             arms,\n1000:             partial,\n1001:         } => {\n1002:             let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n1003:             let mut body = String::new();\n1004:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n1005:                 let mut run = String::new();\n1006:                 if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n1007:                     let variant = arm\n1008:                         .variant\n1009:                         .as_ref()\n1010:                         .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n1011:                     run.push_str(&format!(\n1012:                         \"const {} = {subj}[{}]; \",\n1013:                         local(pid),\n1014:                         js_string(variant)\n1015:                     ));\n1016:                 }\n1017:                 run.push_str(&arm_return(expr(enums, &arm.body), *partial));\n1018:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n1019:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n1020:                         Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] !== undefined\", js_string(v)))\n1021:                     }\n1022:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} === {}\", js_string(v))),\n1023:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n1024:                     (None, None) => None,\n1025:                 };\n1026:                 match test {\n1027:                     Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n1028:                         body.push_str(&format!(\"if ({test}) {{ {run}}} \"));\n1029:                     }\n1030:                     _ => body.push_str(&run),\n1031:                 }\n1032:             }\n1033:             if *partial {\n1034:                 body.push_str(\"return \\\"none\\\"; \");\n1035:             }\n1036:             format!(\"(() => {{ {body}}})()\")\n1037:         }\n1038:     }\n1039: }\n1040: \n1041: /// The node's type picks the literal's representation (kantord/toylang#83): an Int64 literal\n1042: /// is a BigInt literal, exact at every written value where a bare number would already have\n1043: /// rounded past 2^53.\n1044: fn int_lit(ty: &Type, n: i64) -> String {\n1045:     if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n1046:         format!(\"{n}n\")\n1047:     } else {\n1048:         n.to_string()\n1049:     }\n1050: }\n1051: \n1052: /// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. BigInt operators are exact\n1053: /// at any width, so on the 64-bit side only the wrap needs spelling -- Math.imul has no\n1054: /// 64-bit sibling and none is needed.\n1055: fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n1056:     if *ty == Type::Float {\n1057:         // IEEE binary64 is the type itself (ADR 0007), so plain JS operators are the exact\n1058:         // arithmetic, with no wrap to spell. Division by zero is the IEEE answer, Infinity,\n1059:         // which is why there is no `tl_div` guard here unlike the integer widths.\n1060:         match op {\n1061:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n1062:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n1063:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n1064:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"({l} / {r})\"),\n1065:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1066:         }\n1067:     } else if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n1068:         match op {\n1069:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n1070:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n1071:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} * {r})\"),\n1072:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} + {r})\"),\n1073:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} - {r})\"),\n1074:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1075:         }\n1076:     } else {\n1077:         match op {\n1078:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n1079:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n1080:             // Math.imul is the only exact 32-bit multiply here: a plain `*` loses the low\n1081:             // bits once the true product passes 2^53.\n1082:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"Math.imul({l}, {r})\"),\n1083:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"(({l} + {r}) | 0)\"),\n1084:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"(({l} - {r}) | 0)\"),\n1085:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1086:         }\n1087:     }\n1088: }\n1089: \n1090: fn js_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n1091:     match op {\n1092:         BinOp::Eq => \"===\",\n1093:         BinOp::Ne => \"!==\",\n1094:         BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n1095:         BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n1096:         BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n1097:         BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n1098:         other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n1099:     }\n1100: }\n1101: \n1102: fn js_string(s: &str) -> String {\n1103:     let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n1104:     out.push('\"');\n1105:     for c in s.chars() {\n1106:         match c {\n1107:             '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n1108:             '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n1109:             '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n1110:             '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n1111:             '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n1112:             // U+2028 and U+2029 are line terminators in JavaScript but not in JSON, so a string\n1113:             // containing them would end the literal early if written through.\n1114:             c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' || c == '\\u{2028}' || c == '\\u{2029}' => {\n1115:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n1116:             }\n1117:             c => out.push(c),\n1118:         }\n1119:     }\n1120:     out.push('\"');\n1121:     out\n1122: }\n\n(End of file - total 1122 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n/// prefixed.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n\nconst SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_select(src, pred) {\n  const out = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < src.length; i++) if (pred(src[i])) out.push(src[i]);\n  return out;\n}\n\";\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_field(v, k, depth) {\n  if (depth === 0) return v[k];","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_js.rs","text":"use crate::ast::{BinOp, LogicOp};\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding the input value is read into. Unspellable in source, since every source name is\n/// prefixed.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n\nconst SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_select(src, pred) {\n  const out = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < src.length; i++) if (pred(src[i])) out.push(src[i]);\n  return out;\n}\n\";\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_field(v, k, depth) {\n  if (depth === 0) return v[k];\n  const out = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) out[i] = tl_field(v[i], k, depth - 1);\n  return out;\n}\n\";\n\nconst OPT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n// An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{some: v}` present, \\\"none\\\" absent.\n// Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null.\nfunction tl_at(v, i, depth) {\n  if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_at(e, i, depth - 1));\n  const n = v.length;\n  if (i < 0) i = n + i;\n  if (i < 0 || i >= n) return \\\"none\\\";\n  return { some: v[i] };\n}\n\";\n\n// `Array.prototype.slice` already clamps out-of-range bounds and counts negatives from the\n// end, so jq's boundary behaviour is the target's native one; `undefined` is a bound left\n// out, which `.slice` reads as the array's own boundary.\nconst SLICE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth) {\n  if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_slice(e, lo, hi, depth - 1));\n  return v.slice(lo, hi);\n}\n\";\n\nconst TAIL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_tail(v) {\n  if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n  return { some: v.slice(1) };\n}\n\";\n\nconst UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_unwrap(v, depth) {\n  if (depth > 0) return v.map((e) => tl_unwrap(e, depth - 1));\n  if (v === \"none\") { throw new Error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\"); }\n  return v.some;\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"// `|0` is ToInt32: it wraps to 32 bits and truncates toward zero, and V8 folds it away once it\n// knows the value is already a Smi.\nfunction tl_div(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n  return (a / b) | 0;\n}\nfunction tl_rem(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n  return (a % b) | 0;\n}\n\"#;\n\n// An Int64 is a BigInt (kantord/toylang#83): a double is off past 2^53, and BigInt's own `/`\n// and `%` already truncate with the dividend's sign. `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the wrap,\n// including `MIN / -1` back to `MIN`.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"function tl_div64(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0n) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n  return BigInt.asIntN(64, a / b);\n}\nfunction tl_rem64(a, b) {\n  if (b === 0n) { throw new Error(\"toylang: divided by zero\"); }\n  return BigInt.asIntN(64, a % b);\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst COLLECT_HELPER: &str = r#\"// Synchronous, because a toylang expression evaluates to completion and node has no\n// synchronous line reader built in. Reads in fixed chunks off the real fd rather than\n// `readFileSync(0)`, so a line is available to the rest of the program as soon as it arrives\n// rather than only once stdin closes.\nfunction tl_collect_lines() {\n  const fs = require(\"fs\");\n  const out = [];\n  let buf = \"\";\n  const chunk = Buffer.alloc(65536);\n  for (;;) {\n    const n = fs.readSync(0, chunk, 0, chunk.length, null);\n    if (n === 0) break;\n    buf += chunk.toString(\"utf8\", 0, n);\n    let i;\n    while ((i = buf.indexOf(\"\\n\")) !== -1) {\n      out.push(buf.slice(0, i));\n      buf = buf.slice(i + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  if (buf.length > 0) out.push(buf);\n  return out;\n}\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_join(v, f) {\n  const parts = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n  return \\\"[\\\" + parts.join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"]\\\";\n}\n\";\n\n// JS string comparison compares UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint order on any\n// pair straddling a surrogate pair: a BMP character above U+DFFF (e.g. U+E000) sorts below an\n// astral character's lead surrogate even though its codepoint is smaller. Iterating with the\n// string iterator (rather than indexing) steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included.\nconst STR_CMP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_str_cmp(a, b) {\n  const ai = a[Symbol.iterator]();\n  const bi = b[Symbol.iterator]();\n  for (;;) {\n    const x = ai.next();\n    const y = bi.next();\n    if (x.done || y.done) return (x.done ? 0 : 1) - (y.done ? 0 : 1);\n    const cx = x.value.codePointAt(0);\n    const cy = y.value.codePointAt(0);\n    if (cx !== cy) return cx < cy ? -1 : 1;\n  }\n}\n\";\n\n// A record and a payload-carrying variant are both objects, and JS `===` on two objects is\n// identity, so `{a: 1} === {a: 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n// Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n// two spellings of one record type may reach here with their keys in different orders.\nconst EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_eq(a, b) {\n  if (a === b) return true;\n  if (typeof a !== \\\"object\\\" || a === null || typeof b !== \\\"object\\\" || b === null) return false;\n  const keys = Object.keys(a);\n  if (keys.length !== Object.keys(b).length) return false;\n  return keys.every((k) => Object.hasOwn(b, k) && tl_eq(a[k], b[k]));\n}\n\";\n\nconst JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_jsonlines(v, f) {\n  const parts = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) parts.push(f(v[i]));\n  return parts.join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\n}\n\";\n\n// The string iterator steps one codepoint at a time, surrogate pairs included (the same\n// iterator STR_CMP_HELPER uses above), so there is no decoding to get right here.\nconst CHARS_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_chars(s) {\n  return Array.from(s, (c) => c.codePointAt(0));\n}\n\";\n\n// Int and Int64 are different runtime types here -- Number and BigInt -- so `+` cannot mix\n// them and each width needs its own fold. `|0` is the 32-bit wrap (the same ToInt32 ARITH_HELPER\n// relies on); `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)` is the 64-bit one ARITH64_HELPER uses.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_sum(v) {\n  let acc = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = (acc + v[i]) | 0;\n  return acc;\n}\nfunction tl_sum64(v) {\n  let acc = 0n;\n  for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) acc = BigInt.asIntN(64, acc + v[i]);\n  return acc;\n}\n\";\n\n// `>` orders Number and BigInt alike, so one maximum serves both widths.\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nfunction tl_max(v) {\n  if (v.length === 0) return \\\"none\\\";\n  let m = v[0];\n  for (let i = 1; i < v.length; i++) if (v[i] > m) m = v[i];\n  return { some: m };\n}\n\";\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n\n    let used = used_helpers(program);\n    let join = matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Vec(_))\n        || contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)\n        || used.jsonlines;\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n        (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n        (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n        (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n        (used.slice, SLICE_HELPER),\n        (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n        (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n        (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n        (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n        (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n        (used.str_cmp, STR_CMP_HELPER),\n        (used.chars, CHARS_HELPER),\n        (used.sum || used.sum64, SUM_HELPER),\n        (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n        (used.eq, EQ_HELPER),\n    ] {\n        if on {\n            out.push_str(text);\n        }\n    }\n    out.push_str(&printers(program));\n\n    // Function declarations hoist, so a call to one defined further down resolves without the\n    // forward declarations Lua needs. Each backend does what its own target does.\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        let param = f.param.as_deref().map(user);\n        if tir::has_tail_call(&f.name, &f.body) {\n            // The contract: a self-tail-call runs in constant stack. A tail call becomes\n            // `param = arg; continue;` in a loop, so 100k-deep self-recursion cannot blow the\n            // JS stack the way a real call would (kantord/toylang#141).\n            let mut fresh = 0;\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"function {}({}) {{\\n\", user(&f.name), param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default()));\n            out.push_str(\"  while (true) {\\n\");\n            out.push_str(&indent(&indent(&tail_stmts(\n                enums, &f.name, param.as_deref(), &mut fresh, &f.body\n            ))));\n            out.push_str(\"  }\\n}\\n\");\n        } else {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\n                \"function {}({}) {{\\n  return {};\\n}}\\n\",\n                user(&f.name),\n                param.as_deref().unwrap_or_default(),\n                expr(enums, &f.body)\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        out.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n        return out;\n    }\n\n    if program.input.is_some() {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"const {INPUT} = JSON.parse(require(\\\"fs\\\").readFileSync(0, \\\"utf8\\\"));\\n\"\n        ));\n    }\n    if program.inputs.is_some() {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"const {INPUTS} = require(\\\"fs\\\").readFileSync(0, \\\"utf8\\\").split(\\\"\\\\n\\\").filter((l) => l.length > 0).map((l) => JSON.parse(l));\\n\"\n        ));\n    }\n\n    let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n    // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON.\n    if matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink) {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"console.log({body});\\n\"));\n    } else {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"console.log({});\\n\",\n            show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n        ));\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n/// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop reading one line at a time off the\n/// real fd rather than `readFileSync(0)`'s read-everything-first.\n///\n/// `process.stdout.write` is synchronous to a file or TTY on POSIX but asynchronous to a pipe --\n/// queued rather than issued immediately -- and the very next thing this loop does is a\n/// *synchronous* read that blocks the whole event loop, which risks stopping a queued write from\n/// ever reaching the pipe before the process blocks again. `tests/streaming.rs`'s `js_streams`\n/// checks this directly against a live pipe rather than assuming either way.\nfn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n        tir::Source::Inputs => {\n            out.push_str(&read_line_helper());\n            out.push_str(\"for (;;) {\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  const t_line_raw = tl_read_line();\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw === null) break;\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw.length === 0) continue;\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  const t_line = JSON.parse(t_line_raw);\\n\");\n            let elem = program\n                .inputs\n                .as_ref()\n                .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n            (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n        }\n        // A raw line is already the element, blank ones included: `lines` keeps them.\n        tir::Source::Lines => {\n            out.push_str(&read_line_helper());\n            out.push_str(\"for (;;) {\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  const t_line_raw = tl_read_line();\\n\");\n            out.push_str(\"  if (t_line_raw === null) break;\\n\");\n            (\"t_line_raw\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n        }\n        // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n        // the loop body never run, the same answer `tl_range` gives eagerly.\n        tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"const n = {};\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n            out.push_str(\"for (let t_i = 0; t_i < n; t_i++) {\\n\");\n            (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n        }\n    };\n    for stage in &fusion.stages {\n        match stage {\n            tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                current = expr(enums, body);\n                current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n            }\n            tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"  const {} = {};\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"  if (!({})) continue;\\n\", expr(enums, pred)));\n                current = local(*param);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    out.push_str(&format!(\n        \"  console.log({});\\n\",\n        show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0)\n    ));\n    out.push_str(\"}\\n\");\n    out\n}\n\n/// The read-one-line-at-a-time machinery a stdin-backed fused loop needs. A `range` source reads\n/// nothing, so it never emits this.\nfn read_line_helper() -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    out.push_str(\"let tl_stdin_buf = \\\"\\\";\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"let tl_stdin_eof = false;\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"function tl_read_line() {\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"  const fs = require(\\\"fs\\\");\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"  for (;;) {\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    const i = tl_stdin_buf.indexOf(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    if (i !== -1) {\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      const line = tl_stdin_buf.slice(0, i);\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      tl_stdin_buf = tl_stdin_buf.slice(i + 1);\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      return line;\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    }\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    if (tl_stdin_eof) {\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      if (tl_stdin_buf.length === 0) return null;\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      const line = tl_stdin_buf;\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      tl_stdin_buf = \\\"\\\";\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"      return line;\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    }\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    const chunk = Buffer.alloc(65536);\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    const n = fs.readSync(0, chunk, 0, chunk.length, null);\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    if (n === 0) { tl_stdin_eof = true; continue; }\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"    tl_stdin_buf += chunk.toString(\\\"utf8\\\", 0, n);\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"  }\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"}\\n\");\n    out\n}\n\n/// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value, so a record's keys\n/// are known and ordered at compile time, in declaration order. That removes the whole class\n/// of disagreement where one backend enumerates keys in insertion order and another sorts\n/// them, and it is what a native backend will have to do anyway, having no runtime type\n/// information at all.\nfn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n        // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n        Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n        Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n        Type::Str => format!(\"JSON.stringify({value})\"),\n        Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n        // String() on a BigInt is the bare digits, no `n` suffix, so Int64 rides the same arm;\n        // on a Number it is JS's shortest round-trip spelling, which is exactly how a Float\n        // prints (Infinity and NaN come out as their names).\n        Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float | Type::Bool => format!(\"String({value})\"),\n        Type::Vec(elem) => {\n            let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"tl_join({value}, ({e}) => {})\",\n                show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n            let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n            let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"(({v}) => {v} === \\\"none\\\" ? \\\"null\\\" : {})({value})\",\n                show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}.some\"), depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n        // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n        Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n        Type::Record(fields) => {\n            // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n            // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, fty)| {\n                    let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", js_string(name));\n                    let key = js_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n                    format!(\"{key} + {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n                })\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" + [{}].join(\\\",\\\") + \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n/// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key object, so the shape is inspected before\n/// rendering. Which payload follows which key is still the type's knowledge, as everywhere else.\nfn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n    let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n    let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n        variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n    if payloads.is_empty() {\n        return format!(\"JSON.stringify({value})\");\n    }\n    let mut body = String::new();\n    if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n        body.push_str(&format!(\n            \"typeof {n} === \\\"string\\\" ? JSON.stringify({n}) : \"\n        ));\n    }\n    for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n        let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n        let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", js_string(vname));\n        let wrapped = format!(\n            \"({} + {} + \\\"}}\\\")\",\n            js_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n            show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n        );\n        if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n            body.push_str(&format!(\"{read} !== undefined ? {wrapped} : \"));\n        } else {\n            // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n            body.push_str(&wrapped);\n        }\n    }\n    format!(\"(({n}) => {body})({value})\")\n}\n\n/// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n/// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n/// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\nfn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"function {}(v) {{\\n  return {};\\n}}\\n\",\n            ty.show_fn(),\n            show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n        ));\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn contains_vec(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(_) => true,\n        Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| contains_vec(enums, t)),\n        // The Vec arm answers before this one can loop: a self-reference is legal only behind\n        // a Vec, so a recursive enum's first payload hop is one.\n        Type::Enum { .. } => ty::variants(enums, ty)\n            .iter()\n            .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| contains_vec(enums, p))),\n        _ => false,\n    }\n}\n\nfn user(name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"v_{name}\")\n}\n\nfn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n    format!(\"t_{id}\")\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Helpers {\n    select: bool,\n    field: bool,\n    index: bool,\n    slice: bool,\n    unwrap: bool,\n    arith: bool,\n    arith64: bool,\n    collect: bool,\n    jsonlines: bool,\n    tail: bool,\n    str_cmp: bool,\n    chars: bool,\n    sum: bool,\n    sum64: bool,\n    max: bool,\n    eq: bool,\n}\n\n/// Which helper a comparison reaches for, decided by the operator and the operand type rather\n/// than by anything in the tree below it: ordering on a Str has to step by codepoint, and\n/// equality on a composite has to walk the structure.\nfn compare_helpers(op: BinOp, operand: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n    used.str_cmp |=\n        *operand == Type::Str && matches!(op, BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge);\n    used.eq |= operand.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne);\n}\n\n/// Two of the six comparison operators cannot be handed to JS as they are written.\n/// `<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=` on native strings compare UTF-16 code units, which disagrees with codepoint\n/// order across a surrogate pair, so `tl_str_cmp` steps by codepoint instead; equality is\n/// unaffected there (the same codepoints give the same units either way). `===` on two objects\n/// is identity, so equality on a composite walks the structure through `tl_eq`\n/// (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate open question and keeps whatever\n/// JS does with it.\nfn compare(enums: &Enums, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n    if lhs.ty.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n        let call = format!(\"tl_eq({}, {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs));\n        return match op {\n            BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(!{call})\"),\n            _ => call,\n        };\n    }\n    if lhs.ty == Type::Str && matches!(op, BinOp::Lt | BinOp::Le | BinOp::Gt | BinOp::Ge) {\n        return format!(\n            \"(tl_str_cmp({}, {}) {} 0)\",\n            expr(enums, lhs),\n            expr(enums, rhs),\n            js_op(op)\n        );\n    }\n    format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), js_op(op), expr(enums, rhs))\n}\n\n/// `+` on two arrays stringifies and joins them, so a Vec reaches for `.concat` instead.\nfn concat(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(_) => format!(\"{}.concat({})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n        _ => format!(\"({} + {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n    }\n}\n\nfn used_helpers(program: &Program) -> Helpers {\n    fn walk(t: &Tir, used: &mut Helpers) {\n        match &t.kind {\n            Kind::Str(_)\n            | Kind::Int(_)\n            | Kind::Float(_)\n            | Kind::Var(_)\n            | Kind::Local(_)\n            | Kind::Input\n            | Kind::Inputs => {}\n            // The source is what reads stdin now, so it is what needs the helper.\n            Kind::Lines => used.collect = true,\n            Kind::Dsv { .. } => used.collect = true,\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, used)),\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n                fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, used));\n            }\n            // A variant's payload and a call's argument are the same shape here -- one optional\n            // child, recursed into and nothing else -- so they share the arm.\n            Kind::EnumLit { payload: child, .. } | Kind::Call { arg: child, .. } => {\n                if let Some(c) = child {\n                    walk(c, used);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Concat(l, r) | Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n                walk(l, used);\n                walk(r, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Compare { op, lhs: l, rhs: r } => {\n                compare_helpers(*op, &l.ty, used);\n                walk(l, used);\n                walk(r, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n                walk(value, used);\n                walk(body, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Map { source, body, .. } | Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n                walk(source, used);\n                walk(body, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n                used.select = true;\n                walk(source, used);\n                walk(pred, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Field { base, .. } => {\n                used.field |= tir::vec_depth(&base.ty) > 0;\n                walk(base, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n                used.jsonlines |= *which == Builtin::JsonLines;\n                used.tail |= *which == Builtin::Tail;\n                used.chars |= *which == Builtin::Chars;\n                used.str_cmp |=\n                    *which == Builtin::Sort && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Str);\n                used.sum |= *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n                used.sum64 |=\n                    *which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int64);\n                used.max |= *which == Builtin::Max;\n                walk(arg, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Arith { lhs, rhs, .. } => {\n                if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n                    used.arith64 = true;\n                } else if t.ty == Type::Int {\n                    used.arith = true;\n                }\n                walk(lhs, used);\n                walk(rhs, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, used),\n            Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n                used.unwrap = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n                used.index = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n                walk(index, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n                used.slice = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n                if let Some(s) = start {\n                    walk(s, used);\n                }\n                if let Some(e) = end {\n                    walk(e, used);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n                walk(subject, used);\n                for a in arms {\n                    if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                        walk(g, used);\n                    }\n                    walk(&a.body, used);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let mut used = Helpers::default();\n    program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut used));\n    walk(&program.body, &mut used);\n    used\n}\n\n/// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged; total chains return the\n/// body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm (kantord/toylang#62).\nfn arm_return(body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n    if partial {\n        format!(\"return {{some: {body}}}; \")\n    } else {\n        format!(\"return {body}; \")\n    }\n}\n\n/// `t` as statements in the tail position: `return <expr>;` for a base case, and for a tail\n/// call `param = <arg>; continue;` so the loop in the emitted function rewinds instead of\n/// growing the JS stack. Lines come out unindented; `indent` places them under the `while`.\nfn tail_stmts(\n    enums: &Enums,\n    name: &str,\n    param: Option<&str>,\n    fresh: &mut usize,\n    t: &Tir,\n) -> String {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } if func == name => {\n            let assign = param.map_or_else(String::new, |p| {\n                format!(\n                    \"{p} = {};\\n\",\n                    arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n                )\n            });\n            format!(\"{assign}continue;\\n\")\n        }\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"const {} = {};\\n{}\",\n            local(*id),\n            expr(enums, value),\n            tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, body)\n        ),\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } if !partial => {\n            // The subject is read into a temp the way `expr`'s IIFE does, but there is no IIFE\n            // to contain it here, so the name has to be fresh rather than the fixed `subj`.\n            let subj = format!(\"tl_tailsub{}\", *fresh);\n            *fresh += 1;\n            let mut out = format!(\"const {subj} = {};\\n\", expr(enums, subject));\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let mut run = String::new();\n                if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n                    let variant = arm\n                        .variant\n                        .as_ref()\n                        .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                    run.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"const {} = {subj}[{}];\\n\",\n                        local(pid),\n                        js_string(variant)\n                    ));\n                }\n                run.push_str(&tail_stmts(enums, name, param, fresh, &arm.body));\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n                        Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] !== undefined\", js_string(v)))\n                    }\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} === {}\", js_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                match test {\n                    // A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having proved nothing\n                    // else can reach it -- the same rule `expr`'s match arm follows.\n                    Some(test) if i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                        out.push_str(&format!(\"if ({test}) {{\\n{}}}\\n\", indent(&run)));\n                    }\n                    _ => out.push_str(&run),\n                }\n            }\n            out\n        }\n        // A partial match wraps every arm body, so no arm body is a tail position; the whole\n        // match stays an expression, exactly as it would outside tail position.\n        Kind::Match { .. } => format!(\"return {};\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n        _ => format!(\"return {};\\n\", expr(enums, t)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Prepend two spaces to every line. `tail_stmts` returns its lines unindented, and each\n/// nesting level (`while` body, an `if` branch) shifts by one indent.\nfn indent(s: &str) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for line in s.trim_end_matches('\\n').split('\\n') {\n        out.push_str(\"  \");\n        out.push_str(line);\n        out.push('\\n');\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(s) => js_string(s),\n        Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n),\n        Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n),\n        Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n        Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n        Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n        Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n        // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n        // works on the array of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n        Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n        // Same raw lines as `lines`, each split on the delimiter into one row.\n        Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n            \"tl_collect_lines().map((l) => l.split({}))\",\n            js_string(delim)\n        ),\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"{}: {}\", js_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n                .collect();\n            // Parenthesised because a brace opening an arrow function's body is a block, so\n            // `(e) => {a: 1}` is a labelled statement rather than an object.\n            format!(\"({{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n\n        // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n        // variant the single-key object a record already is.\n        Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n            None => js_string(variant),\n            Some(p) => format!(\"({{{}: {}}})\", js_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n        },\n\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n            format!(\"[{}]\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n            \"{}({})\",\n            user(func),\n            arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n        ),\n        Kind::Concat(l, r) => concat(enums, &t.ty, l, r),\n        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n            let op = match op {\n                LogicOp::And => \"&&\",\n                LogicOp::Or => \"||\",\n            };\n            format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs))\n        }\n        Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(!{})\", expr(enums, base)),\n        Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n            Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"String({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // The one real conversion among the backends: an Int is a number and an Int64 is\n            // a BigInt, and BigInt() of a 32-bit integer is always exact.\n            Builtin::IntToI64 => format!(\"BigInt({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Range => {\n                format!(\n                    \"Array.from({{ length: Math.max(0, {}) }}, (_, i) => i)\",\n                    expr(enums, arg)\n                )\n            }\n            Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_jsonlines({}, ({e}) => {})\",\n                    expr(enums, arg),\n                    show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n                )\n            }\n            // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n            Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Length => format!(\"{}.length\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"{}.flat()\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // `Array.prototype.sort`'s default comparator stringifies, which is wrong for\n            // numbers; `tl_str_cmp` already returns the -1/0/1 a comparator wants, so it can be\n            // passed straight through for Str.\n            Builtin::Sort => {\n                let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec\");\n                if *elem == Type::Str {\n                    format!(\"[...{}].sort(tl_str_cmp)\", expr(enums, arg))\n                } else {\n                    // `a - b` would coerce the comparator's result with ToNumber, which\n                    // throws on BigInt -- and Int64 is BigInt here. The three-way compare\n                    // returns plain -1/0/1 for Number and BigInt alike.\n                    format!(\n                        \"[...{}].sort((a, b) => a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0)\",\n                        expr(enums, arg)\n                    )\n                }\n            }\n            // `.reverse()` mutates in place, so the spread copy is what keeps this an ordinary\n            // expression rather than a statement with a visible side effect on `arg`.\n            Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"[...{}].reverse()\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // One fold per width, since the element type picks the runtime integer type.\n            Builtin::Sum => {\n                if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n                    format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n                } else {\n                    format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n                }\n            }\n            Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // The names come from the checked type, not the object value, so `arg` is evaluated\n            // only for whatever else it does (a division inside it must still throw) and its\n            // value discarded with the comma operator.\n            Builtin::Fields => {\n                let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n                    unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n                };\n                let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| js_string(n)).collect();\n                format!(\"({}, [{}])\", expr(enums, arg), names.join(\", \"))\n            }\n        },\n        Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(enums, *op, lhs, rhs),\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"(({}) => {})({})\",\n                local(*id),\n                expr(enums, body),\n                expr(enums, value)\n            )\n        }\n        // Array.prototype.map is exactly this, so no helper is needed.\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"{}.map(({}) => {})\",\n                expr(enums, source),\n                local(*param),\n                expr(enums, body)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_select({}, ({}) => {})\",\n                expr(enums, source),\n                local(*param),\n                expr(enums, pred)\n            )\n        }\n        // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the tagged shape (`\"none\"` or `{some: v}`)\n        // is generic enough that this is the ordinary key-presence test every Match arm over\n        // an Opt subject would already use, just rebuilding the payload instead of a body.\n        Kind::OptMap {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"(__opt => __opt === \\\"none\\\" ? \\\"none\\\" : (({}) => ({{some: {}}}))(__opt.some))({})\",\n                local(*param),\n                expr(enums, body),\n                expr(enums, source)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Index {\n            base, index, depth, ..\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                expr(enums, index),\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Slice {\n            base, start, end, depth,\n        } => {\n            let lo = match start {\n                Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n                None => \"undefined\".to_string(),\n            };\n            let hi = match end {\n                Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n                None => \"undefined\".to_string(),\n            };\n            format!(\n                \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                lo,\n                hi,\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n            if depth == 0 {\n                format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), js_string(name))\n            } else {\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n                    expr(enums, base),\n                    js_string(name),\n                    depth\n                )\n            }\n        }\n        // A chain of tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): string\n        // equality for a unit variant, key presence for a payload variant, the guard's own\n        // Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n        // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tags every present arm and falls\n        // through to the absent Opt.\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } => {\n            let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n            let mut body = String::new();\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let mut run = String::new();\n                if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n                    let variant = arm\n                        .variant\n                        .as_ref()\n                        .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                    run.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"const {} = {subj}[{}]; \",\n                        local(pid),\n                        js_string(variant)\n                    ));\n                }\n                run.push_str(&arm_return(expr(enums, &arm.body), *partial));\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n                        Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] !== undefined\", js_string(v)))\n                    }\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} === {}\", js_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                match test {\n                    Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                        body.push_str(&format!(\"if ({test}) {{ {run}}} \"));\n                    }\n                    _ => body.push_str(&run),\n                }\n            }\n            if *partial {\n                body.push_str(\"return \\\"none\\\"; \");\n            }\n            format!(\"(() => {{ {body}}})()\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The node's type picks the literal's representation (kantord/toylang#83): an Int64 literal\n/// is a BigInt literal, exact at every written value where a bare number would already have\n/// rounded past 2^53.\nfn int_lit(ty: &Type, n: i64) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        format!(\"{n}n\")\n    } else {\n        n.to_string()\n    }\n}\n\n/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. BigInt operators are exact\n/// at any width, so on the 64-bit side only the wrap needs spelling -- Math.imul has no\n/// 64-bit sibling and none is needed.\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Float {\n        // IEEE binary64 is the type itself (ADR 0007), so plain JS operators are the exact\n        // arithmetic, with no wrap to spell. Division by zero is the IEEE answer, Infinity,\n        // which is why there is no `tl_div` guard here unlike the integer widths.\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"({l} / {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} * {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"BigInt.asIntN(64, {l} - {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n            // Math.imul is the only exact 32-bit multiply here: a plain `*` loses the low\n            // bits once the true product passes 2^53.\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"Math.imul({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"(({l} + {r}) | 0)\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"(({l} - {r}) | 0)\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn js_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Eq => \"===\",\n        BinOp::Ne => \"!==\",\n        BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n        BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n        BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n        BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n    }\n}\n\nfn js_string(s: &str) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n    out.push('\"');\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c {\n            '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n            '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n            '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n            '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n            '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            // U+2028 and U+2029 are line terminators in JavaScript but not in JSON, so a string\n            // containing them would end the literal early if written through.\n            c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' || c == '\\u{2028}' || c == '\\u{2029}' => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\u{:04x}\", c as u32));\n            }\n            c => out.push(c),\n        }\n    }\n    out.push('\"');\n    out\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1122,"totalLines":1122,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/emit_js.rs","time":{"start":1788384480554,"end":1788384480562}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the JS backend's approach. Float is currently \"JS-only\" per the backend_js.rs comments. The Lua backend currently has `unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` in emit_lua.rs.\n\nMy task: implement Float for the Lua backend, mirroring the JS host-hook approach. Let me now understand:\n1. How the JS backend emits Float operations (emit_js.rs)\n2. How the Lua backend works (emit_lua.rs)\n3. What \"host hook\" means here\n\nLet me read emit_js.rs thoroughly, focusing on Float.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_064058bbb001IWt8PGpc4W9R6a","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_0640578ea001gnAzW5BX93oe2z"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788384480574,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_064058d3c001tdnc4H3ErPucfb","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"af4fa15ed4996d0bb919f8e70bd0271c9d7c1cdd","messageID":"msg_0640578ea001gnAzW5BX93oe2z","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":28704,"input":2142,"output":70,"reasoning":124,"cache":{"write":0,"read":26368}},"cost":0.000596038}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788384482611,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_064059531001fpJrkriIlkC5w2","messageID":"msg_064058d45001B4piuqXUy0VsKR","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","snapshot":"af4fa15ed4996d0bb919f8e70bd0271c9d7c1cdd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384512839,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b8fc77383200e59b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_lua.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_lua.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: use crate::ast::BinOp;\n2: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n3: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n4: \n5: /// The global the input value is bound to before the chunk runs. Unspellable in source, since\n6: /// every source name is prefixed.\n7: pub const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n8: /// The global every remaining stdin line, already parsed, is bound to before the chunk runs.\n9: pub const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n10: /// The function `run_lua` injects in place of `INPUTS` for a fused, live-streamable program:\n11: /// called with no arguments, it returns the next `inputs` record already parsed and converted,\n12: /// or `nil` at EOF. See `tir::fusion` and this file's `fused_main`.\n13: pub const NEXT_INPUT: &str = \"tl_next_input\";\n14: \n15: const SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n16: local function tl_select(src, pred)\n17:   local out = {}\n18:   for i = 1, #src do\n19:     if pred(src[i]) then out[#out + 1] = src[i] end\n20:   end\n21:   return out\n22: end\n23: \";\n24: \n25: const FIELD_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n26: local function tl_field(v, k, depth)\n27:   if depth == 0 then return v[k] end\n28:   local out = {}\n29:   for i = 1, #v do out[i] = tl_field(v[i], k, depth - 1) end\n30:   return out\n31: end\n32: \";\n33: \n34: const RANGE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n35: local function tl_range(n)\n36:   local out = {}\n37:   for i = 1, n do out[i] = i - 1 end\n38:   return out\n39: end\n40: \";\n41: \n42: const COLLECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n43: local function tl_collect_lines()\n44:   local out = {}\n45:   for line in io.lines() do out[#out + 1] = line end\n46:   return out\n47: end\n48: \";\n49: \n50: // `dsv` splits each collected line on the delimiter. `string.find` with `plain = true` is the\n51: // literal-search form, so the delimiter is not read as a Lua pattern.\n52: const SPLIT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n53: local function tl_split(s, sep)\n54:   local out = {}\n55:   local pos = 1\n56:   while true do\n57:     local start, stop = string.find(s, sep, pos, true)\n58:     if not start then\n59:       out[#out + 1] = string.sub(s, pos)\n60:       break\n61:     end\n62:     out[#out + 1] = string.sub(s, pos, start - 1)\n63:     pos = stop + 1\n64:   end\n65:   return out\n66: end\n67: \n68: local function tl_split_lines(lines, sep)\n69:   local out = {}\n70:   for i = 1, #lines do out[i] = tl_split(lines[i], sep) end\n71:   return out\n72: end\n73: \";\n74: \n75: const MAP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n76: local function tl_map(src, f)\n77:   local out = {}\n78:   for i = 1, #src do out[i] = f(src[i]) end\n79:   return out\n80: end\n81: \";\n82: \n83: const OPT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n84: -- An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{some = v}` present, \\\"none\\\" absent.\n85: -- Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null. A\n86: -- table rather than nil also keeps `#` honest for a Vec of Opt.\n87: local function tl_at(v, i, depth)\n88:   if depth > 0 then\n89:     local out = {}\n90:     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_at(v[k], i, depth - 1) end\n91:     return out\n92:   end\n93:   local n = #v\n94:   if i < 0 then i = n + i end\n95:   if i < 0 or i >= n then return \\\"none\\\" end\n96:   return { some = v[i + 1] }\n97: end\n98: \";\n99: \n100: // Lua has no slice operator, so the clamping is spelled out: negatives count from the end,\n101: // then both bounds clamp to [0, n], and a crossed window is empty. A `nil` bound is one left\n102: // out (0 for the start, n for the end).\n103: const SLICE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n104: local function tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth)\n105:   if depth > 0 then\n106:     local out = {}\n107:     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_slice(v[k], lo, hi, depth - 1) end\n108:     return out\n109:   end\n110:   local n = #v\n111:   local function norm(i)\n112:     if i == nil then return nil end\n113:     if i < 0 then i = n + i end\n114:     if i < 0 then i = 0 end\n115:     if i > n then i = n end\n116:     return i\n117:   end\n118:   lo = norm(lo) or 0\n119:   hi = norm(hi) or n\n120:   local out = {}\n121:   if lo < hi then\n122:     local j = 0\n123:     for i = lo + 1, hi do j = j + 1 out[j] = v[i] end\n124:   end\n125:   return out\n126: end\n127: \";\n128: \n129: const TAIL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n130: local function tl_tail(v)\n131:   if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n132:   local out = {}\n133:   for i = 2, #v do out[i - 1] = v[i] end\n134:   return { some = out }\n135: end\n136: \";\n137: \n138: const FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n139: local function tl_flatten(vv)\n140:   local out = {}\n141:   for i = 1, #vv do\n142:     local inner = vv[i]\n143:     for j = 1, #inner do out[#out + 1] = inner[j] end\n144:   end\n145:   return out\n146: end\n147: \";\n148: \n149: // `table.sort`'s default comparator is Lua's own `<`, which already agrees with every other\n150: // backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\n151: // so nothing here has to branch on the element type.\n152: const SORT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n153: local function tl_sort(v)\n154:   local out = {}\n155:   for i = 1, #v do out[i] = v[i] end\n156:   table.sort(out)\n157:   return out\n158: end\n159: \";\n160: \n161: const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n162: local function tl_reverse(v)\n163:   local out = {}\n164:   local n = #v\n165:   for i = 1, n do out[i] = v[n - i + 1] end\n166:   return out\n167: end\n168: \";\n169: \n170: // `narrow` is true exactly when the element type is Int: both widths live in the same 64-bit\n171: // integer here, so the only difference is whether each addition is brought back to 32 bits the\n172: // way `+` does.\n173: const SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n174: local function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n175:   local acc = 0\n176:   for i = 1, #v do\n177:     acc = acc + v[i]\n178:     if narrow then acc = tl_i32(acc) end\n179:   end\n180:   return acc\n181: end\n182: \";\n183: \n184: const MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n185: local function tl_max(v)\n186:   if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n187:   local m = v[1]\n188:   for i = 2, #v do\n189:     if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n190:   end\n191:   return { some = m }\n192: end\n193: \";\n194: \n195: const UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n196:   if depth > 0 then\n197:     local out = {}\n198:     for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n199:     return out\n200:   end\n201:   if v == \"none\" then error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\", 0) end\n202:   return v.some\n203: end\n204: \"#;\n205: \n206: // A record and a payload-carrying variant are both tables, and Lua's `==` on two tables is\n207: // identity, so `{a = 1} == {a = 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n208: // Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n209: // two spellings of one record type may reach here in different field orders.\n210: const EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n211: local function tl_eq(a, b)\n212:   if a == b then return true end\n213:   if type(a) ~= \\\"table\\\" or type(b) ~= \\\"table\\\" then return false end\n214:   local extra = 0\n215:   for k, v in pairs(a) do\n216:     extra = extra + 1\n217:     if not tl_eq(v, b[k]) then return false end\n218:   end\n219:   for _ in pairs(b) do extra = extra - 1 end\n220:   return extra == 0\n221: end\n222: \";\n223: \n224: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"local TL_2_31 <const> = 2147483648\n225: local TL_2_32 <const> = 4294967296\n226: -- Lua integers are 64-bit, so an Int has to be brought back into 32 bits after every operation.\n227: -- `%` is floored here and the modulus is positive, so this is exact for negatives too.\n228: local function tl_i32(x)\n229:   return (x + TL_2_31) % TL_2_32 - TL_2_31\n230: end\n231: -- Lua's `//` floors and `%` is floored; toylang truncates, which math.fmod already does.\n232: local function tl_div(a, b)\n233:   if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n234:   return tl_i32((a - math.fmod(a, b)) // b)\n235: end\n236: local function tl_rem(a, b)\n237:   if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n238:   return tl_i32(math.fmod(a, b))\n239: end\n240: \"#;\n241: \n242: // Lua's integers already are 64-bit and wrap (kantord/toylang#83), so an Int64's `+`, `-` and\n243: // `*` need nothing at all -- the one backend where the wider type is cheaper than the narrow\n244: // one. Only division needs spelling: `//` floors where toylang truncates, and `math.fmod` is\n245: // already truncated, so the same fmod-then-divide `tl_div` uses works with the `tl_i32` taken\n246: // off. `MIN // -1` wraps back to `MIN` on its own, Lua's documented overflow rule.\n247: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_div64(a, b)\n248:   if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n249:   return (a - math.fmod(a, b)) // b\n250: end\n251: local function tl_rem64(a, b)\n252:   if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n253:   return math.fmod(a, b)\n254: end\n255: \"#;\n256: \n257: const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_quote(s)\n258:   return '\"' .. s:gsub('[%c\"\\\\]', function(c)\n259:     if c == '\"' then return '\\\\\"' end\n260:     if c == '\\\\' then return '\\\\\\\\' end\n261:     if c == '\\n' then return '\\\\n' end\n262:     if c == '\\r' then return '\\\\r' end\n263:     if c == '\\t' then return '\\\\t' end\n264:     return string.format('\\\\u%04x', c:byte())\n265:   end) .. '\"'\n266: end\n267: \"#;\n268: \n269: const JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n270: local function tl_join(v, f)\n271:   local parts = {}\n272:   for i = 1, #v do parts[i] = f(v[i]) end\n273:   return \\\"[\\\" .. table.concat(parts, \\\",\\\") .. \\\"]\\\"\n274: end\n275: \";\n276: \n277: const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n278: local function tl_jsonlines(v, f)\n279:   local parts = {}\n280:   for i = 1, #v do parts[i] = f(v[i]) end\n281:   return table.concat(parts, \\\"\\\\n\\\")\n282: end\n283: \";\n284: \n285: // Lua strings are byte arrays with no Unicode awareness, so decoding by codepoint (rather than\n286: // by byte) has to be hand-rolled, the same as the native runtime's tl_chars does in C.\n287: const CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_chars(s)\n288:   local out = {}\n289:   local i = 1\n290:   local n = #s\n291:   while i <= n do\n292:     local b0 = s:byte(i)\n293:     local cp, extra\n294:     if b0 < 0x80 then cp, extra = b0, 0\n295:     elseif b0 & 0xE0 == 0xC0 then cp, extra = b0 & 0x1F, 1\n296:     elseif b0 & 0xF0 == 0xE0 then cp, extra = b0 & 0x0F, 2\n297:     else cp, extra = b0 & 0x07, 3\n298:     end\n299:     i = i + 1\n300:     for _ = 1, extra do\n301:       cp = (cp << 6) | (s:byte(i) & 0x3F)\n302:       i = i + 1\n303:     end\n304:     out[#out + 1] = cp\n305:   end\n306:   return out\n307: end\n308: \"#;\n309: \n310: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n311:     let enums = &program.enums;\n312:     let mut out = String::new();\n313: \n314:     let used = used_helpers(program);\n315:     // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON. So a\n316:     // string inside a Vec is quoted while a bare string is not.\n317:     let structured = !matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink);\n318:     let quote = (structured && needs_quote(&program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n319:     let join = (structured && contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n320:     for (on, text) in [\n321:         (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n322:         (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n323:         (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n324:         (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n325:         (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n326:         (used.slice, SLICE_HELPER),\n327:         (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n328:         (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n329:         (used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),\n330:         (used.sort, SORT_HELPER),\n331:         (used.reverse, REVERSE_HELPER),\n332:         (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n333:         (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n334:         // After ARITH_HELPER: `tl_sum` narrows through `tl_i32`, and a local declared later in\n335:         // the same chunk is invisible to an earlier function body, which would fall back to the\n336:         // nil global at runtime.\n337:         (used.sum, SUM_HELPER),\n338:         (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n339:         (used.map, MAP_HELPER),\n340:         (used.range, RANGE_HELPER),\n341:         (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n342:         (used.split, SPLIT_HELPER),\n343:         (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n344:         (used.chars, CHARS_HELPER),\n345:         (used.eq, EQ_HELPER),\n346:     ] {\n347:         if on {\n348:             out.push_str(text);\n349:         }\n350:     }\n351: \n352:     out.push_str(&printers(program));\n353: \n354:     // All names are declared before any body, because the checker collects signatures before\n355:     // checking bodies and so accepts a call to a function defined further down. Emitting\n356:     // `local function` in source order would leave that call resolving to a nil global.\n357:     if !program.funcs.is_empty() {\n358:         let names: Vec<String> = program.funcs.iter().map(|f| user(&f.name)).collect();\n359:         out.push_str(&format!(\"local {}\\n\", names.join(\", \")));\n360:     }\n361: \n362:     for f in &program.funcs {\n363:         out.push_str(&format!(\n364:             \"function {}({})\\n  return {}\\nend\\n\",\n365:             user(&f.name),\n366:             f.param.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, user),\n367:             expr(enums, &f.body)\n368:         ));\n369:     }\n370: \n371:     if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n372:         out.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n373:     } else {\n374:         let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n375:         if structured {\n376:             out.push_str(&format!(\n377:                 \"print({})\\n\",\n378:                 show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n379:             ));\n380:         } else {\n381:             out.push_str(&format!(\"print({body})\\n\"));\n382:         }\n383:     }\n384:     out\n385: }\n386: \n387: /// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop over one entry at a time: for an\n388: /// `inputs` source, a call to `NEXT_INPUT` for one already-parsed record (see that constant's\n389: /// doc comment for why the parsing itself is not written here); for `lines`, `io.lines()`\n390: /// itself, exactly as the eager collect helper reads it.\n391: fn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n392:     let enums = &program.enums;\n393:     let mut out = String::new();\n394:     let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n395:         tir::Source::Inputs => {\n396:             out.push_str(\"while true do\\n\");\n397:             out.push_str(&format!(\"  local t_line = {NEXT_INPUT}()\\n\"));\n398:             out.push_str(\"  if t_line == nil then break end\\n\");\n399:             let elem = program\n400:                 .inputs\n401:                 .as_ref()\n402:                 .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n403:             (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n404:         }\n405:         tir::Source::Lines => {\n406:             out.push_str(\"for t_line in io.lines() do\\n\");\n407:             (\"t_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n408:         }\n409:         // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n410:         // Lua's numeric for run zero times (start > limit), the same answer `tl_range` gives\n411:         // eagerly.\n412:         tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n413:             out.push_str(&format!(\"local n = {}\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n414:             out.push_str(\"for t_i = 0, n - 1 do\\n\");\n415:             (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n416:         }\n417:     };\n418:     for stage in &fusion.stages {\n419:         match stage {\n420:             tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n421:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"  local {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n422:                 current = expr(enums, body);\n423:                 current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n424:             }\n425:             tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n426:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"  local {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n427:                 out.push_str(&format!(\n428:                     \"  if not ({}) then goto tl_continue end\\n\",\n429:                     expr(enums, pred)\n430:                 ));\n431:                 current = local(*param);\n432:             }\n433:         }\n434:     }\n435:     // Always the JSON rendering, never the top-level raw-Str rule: each line is one JSON value\n436:     // (that is what jsonlines promises), so a Str element prints quoted here exactly as the\n437:     // eager path's per-element `show` prints it. Only whole-program results print raw.\n438:     let printed = show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0);\n439:     out.push_str(&format!(\"  print({printed})\\n\"));\n440:     out.push_str(\"  ::tl_continue::\\n\");\n441:     out.push_str(\"end\\n\");\n442:     out\n443: }\n444: \n445: /// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value. A Lua table cannot\n446: /// say whether it is an array or a record -- an empty one is indistinguishable either way -- so\n447: /// asking it was always going to disagree with a backend that knows. See emit_js for the same\n448: /// function, and step 4 onwards, where a native target has nothing to ask.\n449: fn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n450:     match ty {\n451:         Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n452:         // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n453:         // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n454:         Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n455:         Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n456:         Type::Str => format!(\"tl_quote({value})\"),\n457:         Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n458:         Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => format!(\"tostring({value})\"),\n459:         Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n460:         Type::Vec(elem) => {\n461:             let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n462:             format!(\n463:                 \"tl_join({value}, function({e}) return {} end)\",\n464:                 show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n465:             )\n466:         }\n467:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n468:             let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n469:             let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n470:             format!(\n471:                 \"(function({v}) if {v} == \\\"none\\\" then return \\\"null\\\" else return {} end end)({value})\",\n472:                 show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}.some\"), depth + 1)\n473:             )\n474:         }\n475:         // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n476:         // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n477:         Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n478:         Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n479:         Type::Record(fields) => {\n480:             // `..` needs an operand on each side, so a record with no fields cannot be\n481:             // built by joining nothing. The other backends survive this by construction.\n482:             if fields.is_empty() {\n483:                 return \"\\\"{}\\\"\".to_string();\n484:             }\n485:             // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n486:             // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n487:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n488:                 .iter()\n489:                 .map(|(name, fty)| {\n490:                     let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", lua_string(name));\n491:                     let key = lua_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n492:                     format!(\"{key} .. {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n493:                 })\n494:                 .collect();\n495:             format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" .. {} .. \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\" .. \\\",\\\" .. \"))\n496:         }\n497:     }\n498: }\n499: \n500: /// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n501: /// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key table, so this is the one printer that has to\n502: /// look before it renders. Only the shape is inspected; which payload follows which key is the\n503: /// type's.\n504: fn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n505:     let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n506:     let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n507:     let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n508:         variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n509:     if payloads.is_empty() {\n510:         return format!(\"tl_quote({value})\");\n511:     }\n512:     let mut body = String::new();\n513:     if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n514:         body.push_str(&format!(\n515:             \"if type({n}) == \\\"string\\\" then return tl_quote({n}) end \"\n516:         ));\n517:     }\n518:     for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n519:         let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n520:         let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", lua_string(vname));\n521:         let wrapped = format!(\n522:             \"({} .. {} .. \\\"}}\\\")\",\n523:             lua_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n524:             show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n525:         );\n526:         if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n527:             body.push_str(&format!(\"if {read} ~= nil then return {wrapped} end \"));\n528:         } else {\n529:             // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n530:             body.push_str(&format!(\"return {wrapped} \"));\n531:         }\n532:     }\n533:     format!(\"(function({n}) {body}end)({value})\")\n534: }\n535: \n536: /// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n537: /// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n538: /// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\n539: fn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n540:     let mut out = String::new();\n541:     for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n542:         out.push_str(&format!(\n543:             \"function {}(v)\\n  return {}\\nend\\n\",\n544:             ty.show_fn(),\n545:             show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n546:         ));\n547:     }\n548:     out\n549: }\n550: \n551: fn needs_quote(ty: &Type) -> bool {\n552:     match ty {\n553:         Type::Str => true,\n554:         Type::Vec(elem) => needs_quote(elem),\n555:         Type::Record(_) => true,\n556:         // A unit variant prints as a quoted string, and a payload wrapper is a record.\n557:         Type::Enum { .. } => true,\n558:         _ => false,\n559:     }\n560: }\n561: \n562: fn contains_vec(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n563:     match ty {\n564:         Type::Vec(_) => true,\n565:         Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| contains_vec(enums, t)),\n566:         // The Vec arm answers before this one can loop: a self-reference is legal only behind\n567:         // a Vec, so a recursive enum's first payload hop is one.\n568:         Type::Enum { .. } => ty::variants(enums, ty)\n569:             .iter()\n570:             .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| contains_vec(enums, p))),\n571:         _ => false,\n572:     }\n573: }\n574: \n575: /// Which identifiers are reserved is the target's business, not toylang's. A program with a\n576: /// function called `print` or `end` would otherwise emit Lua that shadows the output function or\n577: /// does not parse.\n578: fn user(name: &str) -> String {\n579:     format!(\"v_{name}\")\n580: }\n581: \n582: fn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n583:     format!(\"t_{id}\")\n584: }\n585: \n586: #[derive(Default)]\n587: struct Helpers {\n588:     select: bool,\n589:     field: bool,\n590:     index: bool,\n591:     slice: bool,\n592:     unwrap: bool,\n593:     arith: bool,\n594:     arith64: bool,\n595:     map: bool,\n596:     range: bool,\n597:     collect: bool,\n598:     jsonlines: bool,\n599:     tail: bool,\n600:     flatten: bool,\n601:     chars: bool,\n602:     sort: bool,\n603:     reverse: bool,\n604:     sum: bool,\n605:     max: bool,\n606:     eq: bool,\n607:     split: bool,\n608: }\n609: \n610: /// Equality on a composite is structural, which Lua's `==` on two tables is not -- it compares\n611: /// references (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate open question and\n612: /// keeps whatever Lua does with it.\n613: fn compare(enums: &Enums, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n614:     if !lhs.ty.is_composite() || !matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n615:         return format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), lua_op(op), expr(enums, rhs));\n616:     }\n617:     let call = format!(\"tl_eq({}, {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs));\n618:     match op {\n619:         BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(not {call})\"),\n620:         _ => call,\n621:     }\n622: }\n623: \n624: /// Parenthesised because there is more than one operator, and Lua's precedence is not toylang's\n625: /// to rely on. `..` is string-only, so a Vec reaches for the same helper `flatten` uses,\n626: /// wrapped around a two-entry outer table rather than through a second helper.\n627: fn concat(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n628:     match ty {\n629:         Type::Vec(_) => format!(\"tl_flatten({{{}, {}}})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n630:         _ => format!(\"({} .. {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n631:     }\n632: }\n633: \n634: /// Which helper a comparison reaches for, which is a question about the operator and the\n635: /// operand type rather than about the tree below it: Lua's `==` on two tables compares\n636: /// references, so equality on a composite walks the structure instead (kantord/toylang#68).\n637: fn compare_helpers(op: BinOp, operand: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n638:     used.eq |= operand.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne);\n639: }\n640: \n641: /// One helper per builtin whose Lua spelling is a function rather than an operator. `arg.ty` is\n642: /// needed for `sum` alone: `tl_sum` narrows through `tl_i32` when the element is an Int, so the\n643: /// helper that defines it has to be present alongside.\n644: fn builtin_helpers(which: Builtin, arg_ty: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n645:     used.range |= which == Builtin::Range;\n646:     used.jsonlines |= which == Builtin::JsonLines;\n647:     used.tail |= which == Builtin::Tail;\n648:     used.flatten |= which == Builtin::Flatten;\n649:     used.chars |= which == Builtin::Chars;\n650:     used.sort |= which == Builtin::Sort;\n651:     used.reverse |= which == Builtin::Reverse;\n652:     used.sum |= which == Builtin::Sum;\n653:     used.max |= which == Builtin::Max;\n654:     used.arith |= which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(arg_ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n655: }\n656: \n657: fn used_helpers(program: &Program) -> Helpers {\n658:     fn walk(t: &Tir, used: &mut Helpers) {\n659:         match &t.kind {\n660:             Kind::Str(_)\n661:             | Kind::Int(_)\n662:             | Kind::Float(_)\n663:             | Kind::Var(_)\n664:             | Kind::Local(_)\n665:             | Kind::Input\n666:             | Kind::Inputs => {}\n667:             // The source is what reads stdin now, so it is what needs the helper.\n668:             Kind::Lines => used.collect = true,\n669:             Kind::Dsv { .. } => {\n670:                 used.collect = true;\n671:                 used.split = true;\n672:             }\n673:             Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, used)),\n674:             Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n675:                 fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, used));\n676:             }\n677:             Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n678:                 if let Some(p) = payload {\n679:                     walk(p, used);\n680:                 }\n681:             }\n682:             Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n683:                 if let Some(a) = arg {\n684:                     walk(a, used);\n685:                 }\n686:             }\n687:             // Lua's `..` is string-only, so a Vec reaches for the same helper `flatten` uses,\n688:             // wrapped around a two-entry outer table rather than through a second helper.\n689:             Kind::Concat(l, r) => {\n690:                 used.flatten |= matches!(t.ty, Type::Vec(_));\n691:                 walk(l, used);\n692:                 walk(r, used);\n693:             }\n694:             Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n695:                 walk(l, used);\n696:                 walk(r, used);\n697:             }\n698:             Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n699:                 compare_helpers(*op, &lhs.ty, used);\n700:                 walk(lhs, used);\n701:                 walk(rhs, used);\n702:             }\n703:             Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n704:                 walk(value, used);\n705:                 walk(body, used);\n706:             }\n707:             Kind::Map { source, body, .. } => {\n708:                 used.map = true;\n709:                 walk(source, used);\n710:                 walk(body, used);\n711:             }\n712:             Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n713:                 walk(source, used);\n714:                 walk(body, used);\n715:             }\n716:             Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n717:                 used.select = true;\n718:                 walk(source, used);\n719:                 walk(pred, used);\n720:             }\n721:             Kind::Field { base, .. } => {\n722:                 // Depth zero is a plain index and needs no helper.\n723:                 used.field |= tir::vec_depth(&base.ty) > 0;\n724:                 walk(base, used);\n725:             }\n726:             Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n727:                 builtin_helpers(*which, &arg.ty, used);\n728:                 walk(arg, used);\n729:             }\n730:             Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n731:                 if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n732:                     used.arith64 |= matches!(op, BinOp::Div | BinOp::Rem);\n733:                 } else {\n734:                     used.arith = true;\n735:                 }\n736:                 walk(lhs, used);\n737:                 walk(rhs, used);\n738:             }\n739:             Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, used),\n740:             Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n741:                 used.unwrap = true;\n742:                 walk(base, used);\n743:             }\n744:             Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n745:                 used.index = true;\n746:                 walk(base, used);\n747:                 walk(index, used);\n748:             }\n749:             Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n750:                 used.slice = true;\n751:                 walk(base, used);\n752:                 if let Some(s) = start {\n753:                     walk(s, used);\n754:                 }\n755:                 if let Some(e) = end {\n756:                     walk(e, used);\n757:                 }\n758:             }\n759:             Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n760:                 walk(subject, used);\n761:                 for a in arms {\n762:                     if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n763:                         walk(g, used);\n764:                     }\n765:                     walk(&a.body, used);\n766:                 }\n767:             }\n768:         }\n769:     }\n770:     let mut used = Helpers::default();\n771:     program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut used));\n772:     walk(&program.body, &mut used);\n773:     used\n774: }\n775: \n776: /// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged; total chains return the\n777: /// body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm (kantord/toylang#62).\n778: fn arm_return(body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n779:     if partial {\n780:         format!(\"return {{some = {body}}} \")\n781:     } else {\n782:         format!(\"return {body} \")\n783:     }\n784: }\n785: \n786: fn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n787:     match &t.kind {\n788:         Kind::Str(s) => lua_string(s),\n789:         Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n790:         Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n791:         Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n792:         Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n793:         Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n794:         Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n795:         // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n796:         // works on the table of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n797:         Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n798:         Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n799:             \"tl_split_lines(tl_collect_lines(), {})\",\n800:             lua_string(delim)\n801:         ),\n802:         // A record is a table keyed by field name, which is what field access reads.\n803:         Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n804:             let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n805:                 .iter()\n806:                 .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"[{}] = {}\", lua_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n807:                 .collect();\n808:             // Parenthesised because Lua will not index a table constructor: `{...}[\"a\"]` does\n809:             // not parse, and the printer reads every field straight off the value.\n810:             format!(\"({{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n811:         }\n812: \n813:         // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n814:         // variant the single-key table a record already is.\n815:         Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n816:             None => lua_string(variant),\n817:             Some(p) => format!(\"({{[{}] = {}}})\", lua_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n818:         },\n819: \n820:         Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n821:             let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n822:             format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n823:         }\n824:         Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n825:             \"{}({})\",\n826:             user(func),\n827:             arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n828:         ),\n829:         Kind::Concat(l, r) => concat(enums, &t.ty, l, r),\n830:         Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n831:         // Lua's `and`/`or` yield an operand rather than a boolean, which is the same thing here:\n832:         // both operands are already booleans, so whichever one comes back is one.\n833:         Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n834:         Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(not {})\", expr(enums, base)),\n835:         Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n836:             Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"tostring({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n837:             // Lua's integers are 64-bit already; an Int just lives in the low half.\n838:             Builtin::IntToI64 => expr(enums, arg),\n839:             Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n840:             Builtin::Range => format!(\"tl_range({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n841:             Builtin::JsonLines => {\n842:                 let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n843:                 let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n844:                 format!(\n845:                     \"tl_jsonlines({}, function({e}) return {} end)\",\n846:                     expr(enums, arg),\n847:                     show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n848:                 )\n849:             }\n850:             // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n851:             Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n852:             Builtin::Length => format!(\"#{}\", expr(enums, arg)),\n853:             Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n854:             Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tl_flatten({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n855:             Builtin::Sort => format!(\"tl_sort({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n856:             Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"tl_reverse({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n857:             Builtin::Sum => format!(\n858:                 \"tl_sum({}, {})\",\n859:                 expr(enums, arg),\n860:                 tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int)\n861:             ),\n862:             Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n863:             // The names come from the checked type, not the table value, so `arg` runs as the\n864:             // function literal's ignored parameter -- the same IIFE shape `Bind` uses -- purely\n865:             // for whatever else it does.\n866:             Builtin::Fields => {\n867:                 let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n868:                     unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n869:                 };\n870:                 let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| lua_string(n)).collect();\n871:                 format!(\n872:                     \"(function(_) return {{{}}} end)({})\",\n873:                     names.join(\", \"),\n874:                     expr(enums, arg)\n875:                 )\n876:             }\n877:         },\n878:         Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(enums, *op, lhs, rhs),\n879:         // Lua has no expression-level `let`, so the binding becomes a call.\n880:         Kind::Bind {\n881:             local: id,\n882:             value,\n883:             body,\n884:         } => {\n885:             format!(\n886:                 \"(function({}) return {} end)({})\",\n887:                 local(*id),\n888:                 expr(enums, body),\n889:                 expr(enums, value)\n890:             )\n891:         }\n892:         Kind::Map {\n893:             source,\n894:             param,\n895:             body,\n896:         } => format!(\n897:             \"tl_map({}, function({}) return {} end)\",\n898:             expr(enums, source),\n899:             local(*param),\n900:             expr(enums, body)\n901:         ),\n902:         Kind::Select {\n903:             source,\n904:             param,\n905:             pred,\n906:         } => format!(\n907:             \"tl_select({}, function({}) return {} end)\",\n908:             expr(enums, source),\n909:             local(*param),\n910:             expr(enums, pred)\n911:         ),\n912:         // The depth comes from the type on the node below, so it cannot disagree with it, and\n913:         // the emitted helper is told the answer rather than inspecting the value for it.\n914:         Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n915:             format!(\n916:                 \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n917:                 expr(enums, base),\n918:                 tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n919:             )\n920:         }\n921:         // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`.some` shape the\n922:         // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the table instead.\n923:         Kind::OptMap {\n924:             source,\n925:             param,\n926:             body,\n927:         } => format!(\n928:             \"(function(__opt) if __opt == \\\"none\\\" then return \\\"none\\\" else local {} = __opt.some return {{some = {}}} end end)({})\",\n929:             local(*param),\n930:             expr(enums, body),\n931:             expr(enums, source)\n932:         ),\n933:         // A Lua table of records is a table of tables, so collapsing needs no gather here;\n934:         // `elem_is_record` matters only where the columns are stored apart.\n935:         Kind::Index {\n936:             base, index, depth, ..\n937:         } => {\n938:             format!(\n939:                 \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n940:                 expr(enums, base),\n941:                 expr(enums, index),\n942:                 depth\n943:             )\n944:         }\n945:         Kind::Slice {\n946:             base, start, end, depth,\n947:         } => {\n948:             let lo = match start {\n949:                 Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n950:                 None => \"nil\".to_string(),\n951:             };\n952:             let hi = match end {\n953:                 Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n954:                 None => \"nil\".to_string(),\n955:             };\n956:             format!(\n957:                 \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n958:                 expr(enums, base),\n959:                 lo,\n960:                 hi,\n961:                 depth\n962:             )\n963:         }\n964:         Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n965:             let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n966:             if depth == 0 {\n967:                 format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), lua_string(name))\n968:             } else {\n969:                 format!(\n970:                     \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n971:                     expr(enums, base),\n972:                     lua_string(name),\n973:                     depth\n974:                 )\n975:             }\n976:         }\n977:         // A chain of tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): string\n978:         // equality for a unit variant, key presence for a payload variant, the guard's own\n979:         // Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n980:         // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm and falls through\n981:         // to `tl_none`, the absent Opt.\n982:         Kind::Match {\n983:             subject,\n984:             arms,\n985:             partial,\n986:         } => {\n987:             let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n988:             let mut body = String::new();\n989:             for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n990:                 let mut run = String::new();\n991:                 if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n992:                     let variant = arm\n993:                         .variant\n994:                         .as_ref()\n995:                         .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n996:                     run.push_str(&format!(\n997:                         \"local {} = {subj}[{}] \",\n998:                         local(pid),\n999:                         lua_string(variant)\n1000:                     ));\n1001:                 }\n1002:                 run.push_str(&arm_return(expr(enums, &arm.body), *partial));\n1003:                 let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n1004:                     (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n1005:                         Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] ~= nil\", lua_string(v)))\n1006:                     }\n1007:                     (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", lua_string(v))),\n1008:                     (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n1009:                     (None, None) => None,\n1010:                 };\n1011:                 match test {\n1012:                     Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n1013:                         body.push_str(&format!(\"if {test} then {run}end \"));\n1014:                     }\n1015:                     _ => body.push_str(&run),\n1016:                 }\n1017:             }\n1018:             if *partial {\n1019:                 body.push_str(\"return \\\"none\\\" \");\n1020:             }\n1021:             format!(\"(function() {body}end)()\")\n1022:         }\n1023:     }\n1024: }\n1025: \n1026: /// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. At 64 bits Lua's own\n1027: /// integers are the semantics (kantord/toylang#83): `+`, `-` and `*` wrap natively, so only\n1028: /// division and remainder go through a helper.\n1029: fn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n1030:     if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n1031:         match op {\n1032:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n1033:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n1034:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n1035:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n1036:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n1037:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1038:         }\n1039:     } else {\n1040:         match op {\n1041:             BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n1042:             BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n1043:             BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i32({l} + {r})\"),\n1044:             BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i32({l} - {r})\"),\n1045:             BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i32({l} * {r})\"),\n1046:             other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n1047:         }\n1048:     }\n1049: }\n1050: \n1051: fn lua_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n1052:     match op {\n1053:         BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n1054:         BinOp::Ne => \"~=\",\n1055:         BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n1056:         BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n1057:         BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n1058:         BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n1059:         other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n1060:     }\n1061: }\n1062: \n1063: fn lua_string(s: &str) -> String {\n1064:     let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n1065:     out.push('\"');\n1066:     for c in s.chars() {\n1067:         match c {\n1068:             '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n1069:             '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n1070:             '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n1071:             '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n1072:             '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n1073:             // Lua reads \\ddd as a byte, so anything above ASCII has to go through as its\n1074:             // UTF-8 bytes rather than as a codepoint escape.\n1075:             c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n1076:                 out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\{:03}\", c as u32));\n1077:             }\n1078:             c => out.push(c),\n1079:         }\n1080:     }\n1081:     out.push('\"');\n1082:     out\n1083: }\n\n(End of file - total 1083 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"use crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The global the input value is bound to before the chunk runs. Unspellable in source, since\n/// every source name is prefixed.\npub const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n/// The global every remaining stdin line, already parsed, is bound to before the chunk runs.\npub const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n/// The function `run_lua` injects in place of `INPUTS` for a fused, live-streamable program:\n/// called with no arguments, it returns the next `inputs` record already parsed and converted,\n/// or `nil` at EOF. See `tir::fusion` and this file's `fused_main`.\npub const NEXT_INPUT: &str = \"tl_next_input\";\n\nconst SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_select(src, pred)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #src do\n    if pred(src[i]) then out[#out + 1] = src[i] end\n  end","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-build-lua/src/emit_lua.rs","text":"use crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The global the input value is bound to before the chunk runs. Unspellable in source, since\n/// every source name is prefixed.\npub const INPUT: &str = \"t_input\";\n/// The global every remaining stdin line, already parsed, is bound to before the chunk runs.\npub const INPUTS: &str = \"t_inputs\";\n/// The function `run_lua` injects in place of `INPUTS` for a fused, live-streamable program:\n/// called with no arguments, it returns the next `inputs` record already parsed and converted,\n/// or `nil` at EOF. See `tir::fusion` and this file's `fused_main`.\npub const NEXT_INPUT: &str = \"tl_next_input\";\n\nconst SELECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_select(src, pred)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #src do\n    if pred(src[i]) then out[#out + 1] = src[i] end\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst FIELD_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_field(v, k, depth)\n  if depth == 0 then return v[k] end\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do out[i] = tl_field(v[i], k, depth - 1) end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst RANGE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_range(n)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, n do out[i] = i - 1 end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst COLLECT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_collect_lines()\n  local out = {}\n  for line in io.lines() do out[#out + 1] = line end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `dsv` splits each collected line on the delimiter. `string.find` with `plain = true` is the\n// literal-search form, so the delimiter is not read as a Lua pattern.\nconst SPLIT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_split(s, sep)\n  local out = {}\n  local pos = 1\n  while true do\n    local start, stop = string.find(s, sep, pos, true)\n    if not start then\n      out[#out + 1] = string.sub(s, pos)\n      break\n    end\n    out[#out + 1] = string.sub(s, pos, start - 1)\n    pos = stop + 1\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\nlocal function tl_split_lines(lines, sep)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #lines do out[i] = tl_split(lines[i], sep) end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst MAP_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_map(src, f)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #src do out[i] = f(src[i]) end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst OPT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\n-- An Opt is its enum's own runtime shape (ADR 0009): `{some = v}` present, \\\"none\\\" absent.\n-- Tagged, so two levels of absence stay two values; only the printer flattens to null. A\n-- table rather than nil also keeps `#` honest for a Vec of Opt.\nlocal function tl_at(v, i, depth)\n  if depth > 0 then\n    local out = {}\n    for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_at(v[k], i, depth - 1) end\n    return out\n  end\n  local n = #v\n  if i < 0 then i = n + i end\n  if i < 0 or i >= n then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  return { some = v[i + 1] }\nend\n\";\n\n// Lua has no slice operator, so the clamping is spelled out: negatives count from the end,\n// then both bounds clamp to [0, n], and a crossed window is empty. A `nil` bound is one left\n// out (0 for the start, n for the end).\nconst SLICE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_slice(v, lo, hi, depth)\n  if depth > 0 then\n    local out = {}\n    for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_slice(v[k], lo, hi, depth - 1) end\n    return out\n  end\n  local n = #v\n  local function norm(i)\n    if i == nil then return nil end\n    if i < 0 then i = n + i end\n    if i < 0 then i = 0 end\n    if i > n then i = n end\n    return i\n  end\n  lo = norm(lo) or 0\n  hi = norm(hi) or n\n  local out = {}\n  if lo < hi then\n    local j = 0\n    for i = lo + 1, hi do j = j + 1 out[j] = v[i] end\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst TAIL_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_tail(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 2, #v do out[i - 1] = v[i] end\n  return { some = out }\nend\n\";\n\nconst FLATTEN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_flatten(vv)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #vv do\n    local inner = vv[i]\n    for j = 1, #inner do out[#out + 1] = inner[j] end\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `table.sort`'s default comparator is Lua's own `<`, which already agrees with every other\n// backend's ordering on both numbers and (byte-wise, so codepoint-wise for valid UTF-8) strings,\n// so nothing here has to branch on the element type.\nconst SORT_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sort(v)\n  local out = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do out[i] = v[i] end\n  table.sort(out)\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\nconst REVERSE_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_reverse(v)\n  local out = {}\n  local n = #v\n  for i = 1, n do out[i] = v[n - i + 1] end\n  return out\nend\n\";\n\n// `narrow` is true exactly when the element type is Int: both widths live in the same 64-bit\n// integer here, so the only difference is whether each addition is brought back to 32 bits the\n// way `+` does.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n  local acc = 0\n  for i = 1, #v do\n    acc = acc + v[i]\n    if narrow then acc = tl_i32(acc) end\n  end\n  return acc\nend\n\";\n\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \\\"none\\\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n\";\n\nconst UNWRAP_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_unwrap(v, depth)\n  if depth > 0 then\n    local out = {}\n    for k = 1, #v do out[k] = tl_unwrap(v[k], depth - 1) end\n    return out\n  end\n  if v == \"none\" then error(\"toylang: unwrapped a value that is not there\", 0) end\n  return v.some\nend\n\"#;\n\n// A record and a payload-carrying variant are both tables, and Lua's `==` on two tables is\n// identity, so `{a = 1} == {a = 1}` was false here and true on Python (kantord/toylang#68).\n// Structural equality is the ratified answer, so composites walk. Keyed rather than ordered:\n// two spellings of one record type may reach here in different field orders.\nconst EQ_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_eq(a, b)\n  if a == b then return true end\n  if type(a) ~= \\\"table\\\" or type(b) ~= \\\"table\\\" then return false end\n  local extra = 0\n  for k, v in pairs(a) do\n    extra = extra + 1\n    if not tl_eq(v, b[k]) then return false end\n  end\n  for _ in pairs(b) do extra = extra - 1 end\n  return extra == 0\nend\n\";\n\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"local TL_2_31 <const> = 2147483648\nlocal TL_2_32 <const> = 4294967296\n-- Lua integers are 64-bit, so an Int has to be brought back into 32 bits after every operation.\n-- `%` is floored here and the modulus is positive, so this is exact for negatives too.\nlocal function tl_i32(x)\n  return (x + TL_2_31) % TL_2_32 - TL_2_31\nend\n-- Lua's `//` floors and `%` is floored; toylang truncates, which math.fmod already does.\nlocal function tl_div(a, b)\n  if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n  return tl_i32((a - math.fmod(a, b)) // b)\nend\nlocal function tl_rem(a, b)\n  if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n  return tl_i32(math.fmod(a, b))\nend\n\"#;\n\n// Lua's integers already are 64-bit and wrap (kantord/toylang#83), so an Int64's `+`, `-` and\n// `*` need nothing at all -- the one backend where the wider type is cheaper than the narrow\n// one. Only division needs spelling: `//` floors where toylang truncates, and `math.fmod` is\n// already truncated, so the same fmod-then-divide `tl_div` uses works with the `tl_i32` taken\n// off. `MIN // -1` wraps back to `MIN` on its own, Lua's documented overflow rule.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_div64(a, b)\n  if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n  return (a - math.fmod(a, b)) // b\nend\nlocal function tl_rem64(a, b)\n  if b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\", 0) end\n  return math.fmod(a, b)\nend\n\"#;\n\nconst QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_quote(s)\n  return '\"' .. s:gsub('[%c\"\\\\]', function(c)\n    if c == '\"' then return '\\\\\"' end\n    if c == '\\\\' then return '\\\\\\\\' end\n    if c == '\\n' then return '\\\\n' end\n    if c == '\\r' then return '\\\\r' end\n    if c == '\\t' then return '\\\\t' end\n    return string.format('\\\\u%04x', c:byte())\n  end) .. '\"'\nend\n\"#;\n\nconst JOIN_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_join(v, f)\n  local parts = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do parts[i] = f(v[i]) end\n  return \\\"[\\\" .. table.concat(parts, \\\",\\\") .. \\\"]\\\"\nend\n\";\n\nconst JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = \"\\\nlocal function tl_jsonlines(v, f)\n  local parts = {}\n  for i = 1, #v do parts[i] = f(v[i]) end\n  return table.concat(parts, \\\"\\\\n\\\")\nend\n\";\n\n// Lua strings are byte arrays with no Unicode awareness, so decoding by codepoint (rather than\n// by byte) has to be hand-rolled, the same as the native runtime's tl_chars does in C.\nconst CHARS_HELPER: &str = r#\"local function tl_chars(s)\n  local out = {}\n  local i = 1\n  local n = #s\n  while i <= n do\n    local b0 = s:byte(i)\n    local cp, extra\n    if b0 < 0x80 then cp, extra = b0, 0\n    elseif b0 & 0xE0 == 0xC0 then cp, extra = b0 & 0x1F, 1\n    elseif b0 & 0xF0 == 0xE0 then cp, extra = b0 & 0x0F, 2\n    else cp, extra = b0 & 0x07, 3\n    end\n    i = i + 1\n    for _ = 1, extra do\n      cp = (cp << 6) | (s:byte(i) & 0x3F)\n      i = i + 1\n    end\n    out[#out + 1] = cp\n  end\n  return out\nend\n\"#;\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n\n    let used = used_helpers(program);\n    // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON. So a\n    // string inside a Vec is quoted while a bare string is not.\n    let structured = !matches!(program.body.ty, Type::Str | Type::Sink);\n    let quote = (structured && needs_quote(&program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    let join = (structured && contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n        (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n        (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n        (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n        (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n        (used.slice, SLICE_HELPER),\n        (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n        (used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),\n        (used.sort, SORT_HELPER),\n        (used.reverse, REVERSE_HELPER),\n        (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n        (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n        // After ARITH_HELPER: `tl_sum` narrows through `tl_i32`, and a local declared later in\n        // the same chunk is invisible to an earlier function body, which would fall back to the\n        // nil global at runtime.\n        (used.sum, SUM_HELPER),\n        (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n        (used.map, MAP_HELPER),\n        (used.range, RANGE_HELPER),\n        (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n        (used.split, SPLIT_HELPER),\n        (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n        (used.chars, CHARS_HELPER),\n        (used.eq, EQ_HELPER),\n    ] {\n        if on {\n            out.push_str(text);\n        }\n    }\n\n    out.push_str(&printers(program));\n\n    // All names are declared before any body, because the checker collects signatures before\n    // checking bodies and so accepts a call to a function defined further down. Emitting\n    // `local function` in source order would leave that call resolving to a nil global.\n    if !program.funcs.is_empty() {\n        let names: Vec<String> = program.funcs.iter().map(|f| user(&f.name)).collect();\n        out.push_str(&format!(\"local {}\\n\", names.join(\", \")));\n    }\n\n    for f in &program.funcs {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"function {}({})\\n  return {}\\nend\\n\",\n            user(&f.name),\n            f.param.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, user),\n            expr(enums, &f.body)\n        ));\n    }\n\n    if let Some(fusion) = tir::fusion(program) {\n        out.push_str(&fused_main(program, &fusion));\n    } else {\n        let body = expr(enums, &program.body);\n        if structured {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\n                \"print({})\\n\",\n                show(enums, &program.body.ty, &body, 0)\n            ));\n        } else {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"print({body})\\n\"));\n        }\n    }\n    out\n}\n\n/// A stream-typed `jsonlines` program, compiled as a loop over one entry at a time: for an\n/// `inputs` source, a call to `NEXT_INPUT` for one already-parsed record (see that constant's\n/// doc comment for why the parsing itself is not written here); for `lines`, `io.lines()`\n/// itself, exactly as the eager collect helper reads it.\nfn fused_main(program: &Program, fusion: &tir::Fusion) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    let (mut current, mut current_ty) = match fusion.source {\n        tir::Source::Inputs => {\n            out.push_str(\"while true do\\n\");\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"  local t_line = {NEXT_INPUT}()\\n\"));\n            out.push_str(\"  if t_line == nil then break end\\n\");\n            let elem = program\n                .inputs\n                .as_ref()\n                .expect(\"an inputs source recorded its element\");\n            (\"t_line\".to_string(), elem.clone())\n        }\n        tir::Source::Lines => {\n            out.push_str(\"for t_line in io.lines() do\\n\");\n            (\"t_line\".to_string(), Type::Str)\n        }\n        // The bound is evaluated once; the loop counter is the element. A negative bound makes\n        // Lua's numeric for run zero times (start > limit), the same answer `tl_range` gives\n        // eagerly.\n        tir::Source::Range(bound) => {\n            out.push_str(&format!(\"local n = {}\\n\", expr(enums, bound)));\n            out.push_str(\"for t_i = 0, n - 1 do\\n\");\n            (\"t_i\".to_string(), Type::Int)\n        }\n    };\n    for stage in &fusion.stages {\n        match stage {\n            tir::Stage::Map { param, body } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"  local {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                current = expr(enums, body);\n                current_ty = body.ty.clone();\n            }\n            tir::Stage::Select { param, pred } => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"  local {} = {}\\n\", local(*param), current));\n                out.push_str(&format!(\n                    \"  if not ({}) then goto tl_continue end\\n\",\n                    expr(enums, pred)\n                ));\n                current = local(*param);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    // Always the JSON rendering, never the top-level raw-Str rule: each line is one JSON value\n    // (that is what jsonlines promises), so a Str element prints quoted here exactly as the\n    // eager path's per-element `show` prints it. Only whole-program results print raw.\n    let printed = show(enums, &current_ty, &current, 0);\n    out.push_str(&format!(\"  print({printed})\\n\"));\n    out.push_str(\"  ::tl_continue::\\n\");\n    out.push_str(\"end\\n\");\n    out\n}\n\n/// The printer is built from the type rather than by inspecting the value. A Lua table cannot\n/// say whether it is an array or a record -- an empty one is indistinguishable either way -- so\n/// asking it was always going to disagree with a backend that knows. See emit_js for the same\n/// function, and step 4 onwards, where a native target has nothing to ask.\nfn show(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Param(_) => unreachable!(\"params are substituted before emit\"),\n        // The checker refuses a program whose result contains a stream, since there is nothing to\n        // print: a stream has no value, only a promise that collect can redeem.\n        Type::Stream(_) => unreachable!(\"a stream cannot reach the printer\"),\n        Type::Char => unreachable!(\"Char cannot reach the printer, refused by the checker\"),\n        Type::Str => format!(\"tl_quote({value})\"),\n        Type::Sink => unreachable!(\"a sink only ever prints raw, never through the printer\"),\n        Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => format!(\"tostring({value})\"),\n        Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n        Type::Vec(elem) => {\n            let e = format!(\"e{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"tl_join({value}, function({e}) return {} end)\",\n                show(enums, elem, &e, depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty.as_opt().is_some() => {\n            let inner = ty.as_opt().expect(\"guarded\");\n            let v = format!(\"o{depth}\");\n            format!(\n                \"(function({v}) if {v} == \\\"none\\\" then return \\\"null\\\" else return {} end end)({value})\",\n                show(enums, inner, &format!(\"{v}.some\"), depth + 1)\n            )\n        }\n        // A recursive enum prints through a function of its own (`printers`), because expanding\n        // one here has no bottom: its payload leads back to the same type.\n        Type::Enum { .. } if ty::is_recursive(enums, ty) => format!(\"{}({value})\", ty.show_fn()),\n        Type::Enum { .. } => show_enum(enums, ty, value, depth),\n        Type::Record(fields) => {\n            // `..` needs an operand on each side, so a record with no fields cannot be\n            // built by joining nothing. The other backends survive this by construction.\n            if fields.is_empty() {\n                return \"\\\"{}\\\"\".to_string();\n            }\n            // The type's field list is declaration order, so this prints as declared. Field\n            // names are identifiers, so the JSON key needs no escaping and is one literal.\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, fty)| {\n                    let read = format!(\"{value}[{}]\", lua_string(name));\n                    let key = lua_string(&format!(\"\\\"{name}\\\":\"));\n                    format!(\"{key} .. {}\", show(enums, fty, &read, depth + 1))\n                })\n                .collect();\n            format!(\"(\\\"{{\\\" .. {} .. \\\"}}\\\")\", parts.join(\" .. \\\",\\\" .. \"))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// The printer for one enum, inline. One type, two runtime shapes (ADR 0009): a unit variant is\n/// a bare string, a payload variant a single-key table, so this is the one printer that has to\n/// look before it renders. Only the shape is inspected; which payload follows which key is the\n/// type's.\nfn show_enum(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, value: &str, depth: usize) -> String {\n    let variants = ty::variants(enums, ty);\n    let n = format!(\"n{depth}\");\n    let payloads: Vec<&(String, Option<Type>)> =\n        variants.iter().filter(|(_, p)| p.is_some()).collect();\n    if payloads.is_empty() {\n        return format!(\"tl_quote({value})\");\n    }\n    let mut body = String::new();\n    if payloads.len() < variants.len() {\n        body.push_str(&format!(\n            \"if type({n}) == \\\"string\\\" then return tl_quote({n}) end \"\n        ));\n    }\n    for (i, (vname, pty)) in payloads.iter().enumerate() {\n        let pty = pty.as_ref().expect(\"filtered to payload variants\");\n        let read = format!(\"{n}[{}]\", lua_string(vname));\n        let wrapped = format!(\n            \"({} .. {} .. \\\"}}\\\")\",\n            lua_string(&format!(\"{{\\\"{vname}\\\":\")),\n            show(enums, pty, &read, depth + 1)\n        );\n        if i + 1 < payloads.len() {\n            body.push_str(&format!(\"if {read} ~= nil then return {wrapped} end \"));\n        } else {\n            // The last payload variant needs no test: the type says nothing else is left.\n            body.push_str(&format!(\"return {wrapped} \"));\n        }\n    }\n    format!(\"(function({n}) {body}end)({value})\")\n}\n\n/// A named printer for every recursive enum the program prints. The call in `show` above is\n/// what a nested occurrence renders as, so the recursion in the type becomes recursion in the\n/// emitted function rather than in this compiler (kantord/toylang#94).\nfn printers(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    for ty in tir::printed_recursive_enums(program) {\n        out.push_str(&format!(\n            \"function {}(v)\\n  return {}\\nend\\n\",\n            ty.show_fn(),\n            show_enum(&program.enums, &ty, \"v\", 0)\n        ));\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn needs_quote(ty: &Type) -> bool {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Str => true,\n        Type::Vec(elem) => needs_quote(elem),\n        Type::Record(_) => true,\n        // A unit variant prints as a quoted string, and a payload wrapper is a record.\n        Type::Enum { .. } => true,\n        _ => false,\n    }\n}\n\nfn contains_vec(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type) -> bool {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(_) => true,\n        Type::Record(fields) => fields.iter().any(|(_, t)| contains_vec(enums, t)),\n        // The Vec arm answers before this one can loop: a self-reference is legal only behind\n        // a Vec, so a recursive enum's first payload hop is one.\n        Type::Enum { .. } => ty::variants(enums, ty)\n            .iter()\n            .any(|(_, p)| p.as_ref().is_some_and(|p| contains_vec(enums, p))),\n        _ => false,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Which identifiers are reserved is the target's business, not toylang's. A program with a\n/// function called `print` or `end` would otherwise emit Lua that shadows the output function or\n/// does not parse.\nfn user(name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"v_{name}\")\n}\n\nfn local(id: LocalId) -> String {\n    format!(\"t_{id}\")\n}\n\n#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Helpers {\n    select: bool,\n    field: bool,\n    index: bool,\n    slice: bool,\n    unwrap: bool,\n    arith: bool,\n    arith64: bool,\n    map: bool,\n    range: bool,\n    collect: bool,\n    jsonlines: bool,\n    tail: bool,\n    flatten: bool,\n    chars: bool,\n    sort: bool,\n    reverse: bool,\n    sum: bool,\n    max: bool,\n    eq: bool,\n    split: bool,\n}\n\n/// Equality on a composite is structural, which Lua's `==` on two tables is not -- it compares\n/// references (kantord/toylang#68). Ordering on a composite is a separate open question and\n/// keeps whatever Lua does with it.\nfn compare(enums: &Enums, op: BinOp, lhs: &Tir, rhs: &Tir) -> String {\n    if !lhs.ty.is_composite() || !matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne) {\n        return format!(\"({} {} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), lua_op(op), expr(enums, rhs));\n    }\n    let call = format!(\"tl_eq({}, {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs));\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Ne => format!(\"(not {call})\"),\n        _ => call,\n    }\n}\n\n/// Parenthesised because there is more than one operator, and Lua's precedence is not toylang's\n/// to rely on. `..` is string-only, so a Vec reaches for the same helper `flatten` uses,\n/// wrapped around a two-entry outer table rather than through a second helper.\nfn concat(enums: &Enums, ty: &Type, l: &Tir, r: &Tir) -> String {\n    match ty {\n        Type::Vec(_) => format!(\"tl_flatten({{{}, {}}})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n        _ => format!(\"({} .. {})\", expr(enums, l), expr(enums, r)),\n    }\n}\n\n/// Which helper a comparison reaches for, which is a question about the operator and the\n/// operand type rather than about the tree below it: Lua's `==` on two tables compares\n/// references, so equality on a composite walks the structure instead (kantord/toylang#68).\nfn compare_helpers(op: BinOp, operand: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n    used.eq |= operand.is_composite() && matches!(op, BinOp::Eq | BinOp::Ne);\n}\n\n/// One helper per builtin whose Lua spelling is a function rather than an operator. `arg.ty` is\n/// needed for `sum` alone: `tl_sum` narrows through `tl_i32` when the element is an Int, so the\n/// helper that defines it has to be present alongside.\nfn builtin_helpers(which: Builtin, arg_ty: &Type, used: &mut Helpers) {\n    used.range |= which == Builtin::Range;\n    used.jsonlines |= which == Builtin::JsonLines;\n    used.tail |= which == Builtin::Tail;\n    used.flatten |= which == Builtin::Flatten;\n    used.chars |= which == Builtin::Chars;\n    used.sort |= which == Builtin::Sort;\n    used.reverse |= which == Builtin::Reverse;\n    used.sum |= which == Builtin::Sum;\n    used.max |= which == Builtin::Max;\n    used.arith |= which == Builtin::Sum && tir::runtime_elem(arg_ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n}\n\nfn used_helpers(program: &Program) -> Helpers {\n    fn walk(t: &Tir, used: &mut Helpers) {\n        match &t.kind {\n            Kind::Str(_)\n            | Kind::Int(_)\n            | Kind::Float(_)\n            | Kind::Var(_)\n            | Kind::Local(_)\n            | Kind::Input\n            | Kind::Inputs => {}\n            // The source is what reads stdin now, so it is what needs the helper.\n            Kind::Lines => used.collect = true,\n            Kind::Dsv { .. } => {\n                used.collect = true;\n                used.split = true;\n            }\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => items.iter().for_each(|i| walk(i, used)),\n            Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n                fields.iter().for_each(|(_, v)| walk(v, used));\n            }\n            Kind::EnumLit { payload, .. } => {\n                if let Some(p) = payload {\n                    walk(p, used);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Call { arg, .. } => {\n                if let Some(a) = arg {\n                    walk(a, used);\n                }\n            }\n            // Lua's `..` is string-only, so a Vec reaches for the same helper `flatten` uses,\n            // wrapped around a two-entry outer table rather than through a second helper.\n            Kind::Concat(l, r) => {\n                used.flatten |= matches!(t.ty, Type::Vec(_));\n                walk(l, used);\n                walk(r, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Logic { lhs: l, rhs: r, .. } => {\n                walk(l, used);\n                walk(r, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n                compare_helpers(*op, &lhs.ty, used);\n                walk(lhs, used);\n                walk(rhs, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Bind { value, body, .. } => {\n                walk(value, used);\n                walk(body, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Map { source, body, .. } => {\n                used.map = true;\n                walk(source, used);\n                walk(body, used);\n            }\n            Kind::OptMap { source, body, .. } => {\n                walk(source, used);\n                walk(body, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Select { source, pred, .. } => {\n                used.select = true;\n                walk(source, used);\n                walk(pred, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Field { base, .. } => {\n                // Depth zero is a plain index and needs no helper.\n                used.field |= tir::vec_depth(&base.ty) > 0;\n                walk(base, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n                builtin_helpers(*which, &arg.ty, used);\n                walk(arg, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => {\n                if t.ty == Type::Int64 {\n                    used.arith64 |= matches!(op, BinOp::Div | BinOp::Rem);\n                } else {\n                    used.arith = true;\n                }\n                walk(lhs, used);\n                walk(rhs, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Not(base) => walk(base, used),\n            Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n                used.unwrap = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Index { base, index, .. } => {\n                used.index = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n                walk(index, used);\n            }\n            Kind::Slice { base, start, end, .. } => {\n                used.slice = true;\n                walk(base, used);\n                if let Some(s) = start {\n                    walk(s, used);\n                }\n                if let Some(e) = end {\n                    walk(e, used);\n                }\n            }\n            Kind::Match { subject, arms, .. } => {\n                walk(subject, used);\n                for a in arms {\n                    if let Some(g) = &a.guard {\n                        walk(g, used);\n                    }\n                    walk(&a.body, used);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    let mut used = Helpers::default();\n    program.funcs.iter().for_each(|f| walk(&f.body, &mut used));\n    walk(&program.body, &mut used);\n    used\n}\n\n/// A partial chain's yield is an Opt, so a present arm is tagged; total chains return the\n/// body bare. Split out of `expr`'s match arm (kantord/toylang#62).\nfn arm_return(body: String, partial: bool) -> String {\n    if partial {\n        format!(\"return {{some = {body}}} \")\n    } else {\n        format!(\"return {body} \")\n    }\n}\n\nfn expr(enums: &Enums, t: &Tir) -> String {\n    match &t.kind {\n        Kind::Str(s) => lua_string(s),\n        Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string(),\n        Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n        Kind::Var(name) => user(name),\n        Kind::Local(id) => local(*id),\n        Kind::Input => INPUT.to_string(),\n        Kind::Inputs => INPUTS.to_string(),\n        // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n        // works on the table of its entries. Fusion is what will remove this materialization.\n        Kind::Lines => \"tl_collect_lines()\".to_string(),\n        Kind::Dsv { delim } => format!(\n            \"tl_split_lines(tl_collect_lines(), {})\",\n            lua_string(delim)\n        ),\n        // A record is a table keyed by field name, which is what field access reads.\n        Kind::RecordLit { fields } => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = fields\n                .iter()\n                .map(|(name, value)| format!(\"[{}] = {}\", lua_string(name), expr(enums, value)))\n                .collect();\n            // Parenthesised because Lua will not index a table constructor: `{...}[\"a\"]` does\n            // not parse, and the printer reads every field straight off the value.\n            format!(\"({{{}}})\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n\n        // The value is its JSON shape: a unit variant is the variant-name string, a payload\n        // variant the single-key table a record already is.\n        Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => match payload {\n            None => lua_string(variant),\n            Some(p) => format!(\"({{[{}] = {}}})\", lua_string(variant), expr(enums, p)),\n        },\n\n        Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n            let parts: Vec<String> = items.iter().map(|i| expr(enums, i)).collect();\n            format!(\"{{{}}}\", parts.join(\", \"))\n        }\n        Kind::Call { func, arg } => format!(\n            \"{}({})\",\n            user(func),\n            arg.as_deref().map_or_else(String::new, |a| expr(enums, a))\n        ),\n        Kind::Concat(l, r) => concat(enums, &t.ty, l, r),\n        Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(&t.ty, *op, expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        // Lua's `and`/`or` yield an operand rather than a boolean, which is the same thing here:\n        // both operands are already booleans, so whichever one comes back is one.\n        Kind::Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => format!(\"({} {op} {})\", expr(enums, lhs), expr(enums, rhs)),\n        Kind::Not(base) => format!(\"(not {})\", expr(enums, base)),\n        Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => match which {\n            Builtin::IntToStr => format!(\"tostring({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // Lua's integers are 64-bit already; an Int just lives in the low half.\n            Builtin::IntToI64 => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Chars => format!(\"tl_chars({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Range => format!(\"tl_range({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::JsonLines => {\n                let elem = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).expect(\"checked to be a Vec or a stream\");\n                let e = \"e0\".to_string();\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_jsonlines({}, function({e}) return {} end)\",\n                    expr(enums, arg),\n                    show(enums, elem, &e, 1)\n                )\n            }\n            // The source already materialized, so the exit has nothing left to do.\n            Builtin::Collect => expr(enums, arg),\n            Builtin::Length => format!(\"#{}\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Tail => format!(\"tl_tail({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Flatten => format!(\"tl_flatten({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Sort => format!(\"tl_sort({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Reverse => format!(\"tl_reverse({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            Builtin::Sum => format!(\n                \"tl_sum({}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, arg),\n                tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int)\n            ),\n            Builtin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n            // The names come from the checked type, not the table value, so `arg` runs as the\n            // function literal's ignored parameter -- the same IIFE shape `Bind` uses -- purely\n            // for whatever else it does.\n            Builtin::Fields => {\n                let Type::Record(fields) = &arg.ty else {\n                    unreachable!(\"checked to be a record\")\n                };\n                let names: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(n, _)| lua_string(n)).collect();\n                format!(\n                    \"(function(_) return {{{}}} end)({})\",\n                    names.join(\", \"),\n                    expr(enums, arg)\n                )\n            }\n        },\n        Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(enums, *op, lhs, rhs),\n        // Lua has no expression-level `let`, so the binding becomes a call.\n        Kind::Bind {\n            local: id,\n            value,\n            body,\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"(function({}) return {} end)({})\",\n                local(*id),\n                expr(enums, body),\n                expr(enums, value)\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Map {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"tl_map({}, function({}) return {} end)\",\n            expr(enums, source),\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, body)\n        ),\n        Kind::Select {\n            source,\n            param,\n            pred,\n        } => format!(\n            \"tl_select({}, function({}) return {} end)\",\n            expr(enums, source),\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, pred)\n        ),\n        // The depth comes from the type on the node below, so it cannot disagree with it, and\n        // the emitted helper is told the answer rather than inspecting the value for it.\n        Kind::Unwrap { base } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_unwrap({}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                tir::vec_depth(&base.ty)\n            )\n        }\n        // Opt's reorder pass (kantord/toylang#66): the same `== \"none\"`/`.some` shape the\n        // printer and Match already read, generalised to rebuild the table instead.\n        Kind::OptMap {\n            source,\n            param,\n            body,\n        } => format!(\n            \"(function(__opt) if __opt == \\\"none\\\" then return \\\"none\\\" else local {} = __opt.some return {{some = {}}} end end)({})\",\n            local(*param),\n            expr(enums, body),\n            expr(enums, source)\n        ),\n        // A Lua table of records is a table of tables, so collapsing needs no gather here;\n        // `elem_is_record` matters only where the columns are stored apart.\n        Kind::Index {\n            base, index, depth, ..\n        } => {\n            format!(\n                \"tl_at({}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                expr(enums, index),\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Slice {\n            base, start, end, depth,\n        } => {\n            let lo = match start {\n                Some(s) => expr(enums, s),\n                None => \"nil\".to_string(),\n            };\n            let hi = match end {\n                Some(e) => expr(enums, e),\n                None => \"nil\".to_string(),\n            };\n            format!(\n                \"tl_slice({}, {}, {}, {})\",\n                expr(enums, base),\n                lo,\n                hi,\n                depth\n            )\n        }\n        Kind::Field { base, name } => {\n            let depth = tir::vec_depth(&base.ty);\n            if depth == 0 {\n                format!(\"{}[{}]\", expr(enums, base), lua_string(name))\n            } else {\n                format!(\n                    \"tl_field({}, {}, {})\",\n                    expr(enums, base),\n                    lua_string(name),\n                    depth\n                )\n            }\n        }\n        // A chain of tests over the subject (a plain local, so re-reading it is free): string\n        // equality for a unit variant, key presence for a payload variant, the guard's own\n        // Bool for a guard arm. A total chain's last arm needs no test, the checker having\n        // proved nothing else can reach it; a partial chain tests every arm and falls through\n        // to `tl_none`, the absent Opt.\n        Kind::Match {\n            subject,\n            arms,\n            partial,\n        } => {\n            let subj = expr(enums, subject);\n            let mut body = String::new();\n            for (i, arm) in arms.iter().enumerate() {\n                let mut run = String::new();\n                if let Some(pid) = arm.payload {\n                    let variant = arm\n                        .variant\n                        .as_ref()\n                        .expect(\"only a variant arm has a payload\");\n                    run.push_str(&format!(\n                        \"local {} = {subj}[{}] \",\n                        local(pid),\n                        lua_string(variant)\n                    ));\n                }\n                run.push_str(&arm_return(expr(enums, &arm.body), *partial));\n                let test = match (&arm.variant, &arm.guard) {\n                    (Some(v), _) if arm.payload.is_some() => {\n                        Some(format!(\"{subj}[{}] ~= nil\", lua_string(v)))\n                    }\n                    (Some(v), _) => Some(format!(\"{subj} == {}\", lua_string(v))),\n                    (None, Some(g)) => Some(expr(enums, g)),\n                    (None, None) => None,\n                };\n                match test {\n                    Some(test) if *partial || i + 1 < arms.len() => {\n                        body.push_str(&format!(\"if {test} then {run}end \"));\n                    }\n                    _ => body.push_str(&run),\n                }\n            }\n            if *partial {\n                body.push_str(\"return \\\"none\\\" \");\n            }\n            format!(\"(function() {body}end)()\")\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// One arithmetic expression at the width the node's type names. At 64 bits Lua's own\n/// integers are the semantics (kantord/toylang#83): `+`, `-` and `*` wrap natively, so only\n/// division and remainder go through a helper.\nfn arith(ty: &Type, op: BinOp, l: String, r: String) -> String {\n    if *ty == Type::Int64 {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem64({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"({l} * {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    } else {\n        match op {\n            BinOp::Div => format!(\"tl_div({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Rem => format!(\"tl_rem({l}, {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Add => format!(\"tl_i32({l} + {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Sub => format!(\"tl_i32({l} - {r})\"),\n            BinOp::Mul => format!(\"tl_i32({l} * {r})\"),\n            other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not arithmetic\"),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn lua_op(op: BinOp) -> &'static str {\n    match op {\n        BinOp::Eq => \"==\",\n        BinOp::Ne => \"~=\",\n        BinOp::Lt => \"<\",\n        BinOp::Le => \"<=\",\n        BinOp::Gt => \">\",\n        BinOp::Ge => \">=\",\n        other => unreachable!(\"{other} is not a comparison\"),\n    }\n}\n\nfn lua_string(s: &str) -> String {\n    let mut out = String::with_capacity(s.len() + 2);\n    out.push('\"');\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match c {\n            '\"' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\"\"),\n            '\\\\' => out.push_str(\"\\\\\\\\\"),\n            '\\n' => out.push_str(\"\\\\n\"),\n            '\\r' => out.push_str(\"\\\\r\"),\n            '\\t' => out.push_str(\"\\\\t\"),\n            // Lua reads \\ddd as a byte, so anything above ASCII has to go through as its\n            // UTF-8 bytes rather than as a codepoint escape.\n            c if (c as u32) < 0x20 || c == '\\u{7f}' => {\n                out.push_str(&format!(\"\\\\{:03}\", c as u32));\n            }\n            c => out.push(c),\n        }\n    }\n    out.push('\"');\n    out\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1083,"totalLines":1083,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788384512825,"end":1788384512834}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the JS backend's Float handling:\n\n1. `Kind::Float(n)` in expr emits `crate::float::lit(*n)` — the literal spelling.\n2. `arith()` handles `Type::Float` with plain operators (+ - * /), no guards.\n3. `show()` for `Type::Float` uses `String({value})` — which produces JS's shortest round-trip spelling, plus \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\" names.\n4. `compare()` — plain comparison operators work on Float.\n5. In `used_helpers` walk, `Kind::Float(_)` is in the leaf arm.\n6. `Backend::Lua` currently has `unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\")` at lines 459, 790 in emit_lua.rs.\n\nNow I need to understand:\n1. How the Lua backend is structured (emit_lua.rs) — how does it do arithmetic, printing, etc.\n2. What \"host hook\" means here. The brief says \"grep for how the JS backend host-hooks Float today and mirror the same host-hook approach for Lua.\"\n\nWait — the JS backend doesn't seem to \"host-hook\" Float in the emitted code. It uses plain JS operators. But maybe \"host hook\" means the fact that Float is natively supported by the target language (the \"host\" language/runtime), as opposed to emulating it. In JS, Float is the native Number. In Lua, Float is the native number type too (Lua numbers are doubles by default). So the \"host hook\" approach is: use the target language's native float support rather than emulating.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The brief says: \"Follow float-build's existing JS implementation as the reference pattern -- find it in src/ (grep for how the JS backend host-hooks Float today) and mirror the same host-hook approach for Lua.\"\n\nSo the JS implementation IS the \"host-hook\" reference. In JS, Float literals are emitted as plain JS number literals (via float::lit), arithmetic uses plain JS operators, printing uses String() which gives shortest round-trip, and NaN/Infinity come from the host's IEEE arithmetic. That's the \"host hook\": the target language's native IEEE 754 double is the implementation of toylang's Float.\n\nFor Lua: Lua numbers are IEEE 754 doubles by default (Lua 5.3+ has integers and floats as subtypes, but with `#!lua` default... actually Lua 5.3+ distinguishes integers and floats but both are subtypes of \"number\"). The default Lua number is a double. When a literal has a decimal point or exponent, it's a float. Arithmetic on floats gives floats. Division always gives floats. NaN/Infinity print as \"nan\"/\"inf\" in standard Lua 5.3+.\n\nWait, important: In Lua, how does `1/0` behave? In standard Lua, `1/0` = `inf`. And `0/0` = `nan`. Print of `inf` is \"inf\" and `nan` is \"-nan\" or \"nan\" depending on sign. But our Float printing needs \"Infinity\" and \"NaN\" byte-for-byte across backends! \n\nHold on. Let me re-read the issue. The acceptance criteria says: \"NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\". The JS test expects `Infinity\\n` and `NaN\\n` and `-Infinity\\n`.\n\nBut if Lua prints `inf` and `nan`, that wouldn't agree byte-for-byte. Hmm. So for the Lua backend, we need the printing to produce \"Infinity\", \"NaN\", \"-Infinity\" like JS.\n\nWait, but how do backends print? Let me look at how the Lua backend prints values. The printer in JS uses `show()` which uses `String(value)` for Float. For Lua, we need an equivalent that produces the same output: shortest round-trip decimal, and \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\"/\"-Infinity\" names.\n\nLua's default `tostring` on a float gives shortest round-trip too? Lua 5.3+ uses \"%.14g\" by default for floats, which is NOT always shortest round-trip. Actually Lua's default formatting for floats is `%.14g`, which produces 14 significant digits, not necessarily the shortest round-trip. This could disagree with JS's shortest round-trip. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — this is a real concern. Let me check what the JS Float backend's snapshot/agreement tests require. The JS backend is currently the ONLY backend with Float. When Lua joins, if they need to agree byte for byte, the printing matters.\n\nLet me think about how the corpus agreement harness works. Let me look at how the corpus runs and how output agreement is checked. But first, let me check the Lua backend's current structure and how it prints.\n\nAlso — I need to figure out how the Lua backend is tested. Is there a Lua runtime installed? The backend needs to run Lua to execute programs. Let me check.\n\nLet me read emit_lua.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0640609b5001UKzoQFTUug8PHv","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064058d45001B4piuqXUy0VsKR"}}
