the \"host hook\" works for JS and what the parallel is for Lua.\n\nKey facts:\n- In JS, Float is the native Number type. Float literals emit via `crate::float::lit()` which is Rust's `f64::to_string()` — shortest round-trip decimal that JS's `String(number)` also produces.\n- JS arithmetic on Float uses plain JS operators.\n- JS printing of Float uses `String(value)` — producing shortest round-trip, \"Infinity\", \"NaN\", \"-Infinity\".\n- JS input: JSON numbers parse to Number doubles.\n\nFor Lua:\n- Lua's native number type is a double (Lua 5.3+, floats are a subtype of number; but by default Lua 5.3/5.4's number is double; integers and floats are both \"number\" subtype).\n- Actually wait — need to check which Lua version the repo's harness runs. Let me check how the Lua backend is executed (src/lib.rs or tests/support). Let me look for where Lua is run. There's probably a `lua` binary or the like.\n\nLet me check the harness: src/lib.rs, and tests/support.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0640625780011Rqoiq1y3QQB0Y","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064060b5d001JKIw5WInUc0VLu"}}
