{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384535077,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_e8bf41312af941688b683d13","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git log --all --oneline | rg -i \"float\" | head -30"},"output":"e6095c5 Drive tick: dispatch float-build-lua (gh:149) into free lane\nc490b98 Apply escalation rulings for issue-168, float-build-149 siblings, issue-153\n4c1a55e opencode-rollout: diagnose mutation-semantics-spike and float-build-lua 2-run stalls, rebrief both\n5e2fd24 Dispatch trait-interface-build (gh:174); escalate 5-run issue-168 offload.rs stall and 6-run float-build-{go,python,rust} stall\n5c446c5 board: dispatch float-build-go into free lane\nafa7de2 board: dispatch float-build-python into free lane\n245bfcc board: dispatch float-build-rust into free lane\n35f0748 board: archive float-build (landed as 0c29911, issue-149 JS Float backend)\nf22a806 Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n1f71b24 board: reshape float-build after 3 commitless runs -- scope to JS backend only\nd42e1b0 Drive tick: unstick float-build's stale needs, dispatch two lanes, rebrief issue-139\n745c1d5 Apply euler-ergonomics and float-and-benchmarks grill round rulings\n3128a21 Drop ESCALATION.md: n-body/spectral-norm scope call captured as board row benchmark-float-tasks-scope\nca7d3fa Write the Float decision into the draft, and track what it left open\n016f2cd Record the Int decision, and decide Float is JavaScript's double\n","metadata":{"output":"e6095c5 Drive tick: dispatch float-build-lua (gh:149) into free lane\nc490b98 Apply escalation rulings for issue-168, float-build-149 siblings, issue-153\n4c1a55e opencode-rollout: diagnose mutation-semantics-spike and float-build-lua 2-run stalls, rebrief both\n5e2fd24 Dispatch trait-interface-build (gh:174); escalate 5-run issue-168 offload.rs stall and 6-run float-build-{go,python,rust} stall\n5c446c5 board: dispatch float-build-go into free lane\nafa7de2 board: dispatch float-build-python into free lane\n245bfcc board: dispatch float-build-rust into free lane\n35f0748 board: archive float-build (landed as 0c29911, issue-149 JS Float backend)\nf22a806 Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n1f71b24 board: reshape float-build after 3 commitless runs -- scope to JS backend only\nd42e1b0 Drive tick: unstick float-build's stale needs, dispatch two lanes, rebrief issue-139\n745c1d5 Apply euler-ergonomics and float-and-benchmarks grill round rulings\n3128a21 Drop ESCALATION.md: n-body/spectral-norm scope call captured as board row benchmark-float-tasks-scope\nca7d3fa Write the Float decision into the draft, and track what it left open\n016f2cd Record the Int decision, and decide Float is JavaScript's double\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git log --all --oneline | rg -i \"float\" | head -30","time":{"start":1788384535073,"end":1788384535075}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Lua backend runs mlua with Lua 5.4 (lua54 feature) vendored.\n\nNow the critical printing concern: how does mlua/Lua 5.4 format a float when `tostring(x)` is called? Lua's default `tostring` on floats uses `%.14g`. That produces e.g. `1.5` → \"1.5\", but `0.1+0.2` might be \"0.3\" — wait, `%.14g` of 0.30000000000000004 gives \"0.3\". While JS's String(0.1+0.2) gives \"0.30000000000000004\". These differ!\n\nSo if we naively emit `tostring(x)` for a Float, we'd disagree with the JS backend byte-for-byte on values whose shortest round-trip repr exceeds 14 sig digits.\n\nHmm, so what does the JS backend's approach require of Lua? The issue says: \"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a format and hold every backend to it.\"\n\nSo the format decision was made during the JS implementation. The float.rs doc says: \"Rust's `Display` for `f64` is the shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double -- exactly what a literal needs to be exact on every target, and what JS's own `String(number)` produces for the same value.\" So the chosen format is the shortest round-trip decimal (Rust Display / JS String / Python repr / etc.).\n\nSo for Lua, to produce the same byte-for-byte output, we need a \"shortest round-trip decimal\" formatting. Lua's default `%.14g` is NOT always that. E.g. Lua 5.4 `tostring(0.1+0.2)` → \"0.3\" (14 sig digits enough to distinguish... wait no. 0.1+0.2 in binary64 = 0.30000000000000004, and \"0.3\" round-trips back to... hmm actually \"0.3\" parses back to 0.29999999999999998... no. 0.3 in binary is 0.299999999999999988897769753748... which is NOT the same double as 0.30000000000000004. So \"0.3\" does NOT round-trip to 0.1+0.2. So `%.14g` would produce a non-round-tripping, non-agreeing output. Actually wait — Lua's `%.14g` of 0.30000000000000004... Let me think: `%.14g` uses 14 significant digits. 0.30000000000000004 → 14 sig digits: 0.30000000000000. And `%g` strips trailing zeros → \"0.3\". Yes.\n\nSo we cannot use Lua's default tostring for Float printing if we need JS agreement. We need a shortest-round-trip formatting. But wait — maybe the agreement harness doesn't compare Float output byte-for-byte across backends unless they're both in the corpus. But the task says \"mirror the same host-hook approach\". And the whole point of corpus cases: \"output means they all print it and agree\". Once Float reaches Lua, float corpus cases become possible, and they'd need agreement.\n\n Actually wait — the JS tests in backend_js.rs are JS-only because \"Float is JS-only in this row: the other backends do not carry it yet, so none of these can be a corpus case, which would require every backend to agree.\" Once Lua carries Float too, we could add corpus cases — but then EVERY backend must agree, and the other five backends (Go, Py, Rust, Native/LLVM, jq) do NOT carry Float yet. So a float corpus case would fail on the five that don't carry it. So we can't add float corpus cases until every backend carries Float.\n\n Hmm, but the acceptance criteria in issue 149 says \"corpus test coverage added for the above\". That's for the whole float-build project across all backends. For THIS lane (Lua backend only), the definition of done is: \"implementation complete; `just check` green\". So I need to make the Lua backend carry Float, and probably move/extend the tests. The backend_js.rs tests are JS-only with a comment explaining Float is JS-only. Now that Lua joins, those tests should cover both Lua and JS.\n\n Actually, let me think about the test harness. The backend_js.rs `agree()` helper runs all backends. But the float tests there use `toylang::run_on(src, stdin, Backend::Js)` directly. When Lua joins, should I add Lua coverage? The brief says \"Implement Float ... for the Lua backend only. ... Follow float-build's existing JS implementation as the reference pattern ... and mirror the same host-hook approach for Lua. One backend, one commit.\"\n\nSo the minimal scope: make the Lua backend emit and run Float programs correctly. The JS tests stay as they are (or perhaps extend to also test Lua?). Let me look at how the previous backends' float-build lanes were done — e.g., float-build-go, float-build-python, float-build-rust lanes — but those are \"todo\" on the board. Actually the board says: float-build-lua (mine), float-build-jq, float-build-c-escalation — all todo. The JS one (float-build) was archived as landed (0c29911). issue-149). So I'm the first follow-up. So there's no existing pattern for how a follow-up lane was done. Hmm.\n\nWait — actually let me check git log for other backends. The git log said \"board: dispatch float-build-go into free lane\", \"board: dispatch float-build-python\", \"board: dispatch float-build-rust\". So those lanes were started by the board but maybe stalled. Let me check if there are commits for those lanes in git history. Let me look at the full git log.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0640661f3001h7xCxOInS4QPgh","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064063091001BP6Q8Ko75dxYk7"}}
