{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384558547,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_e35efa0aa0b842aca7a3d162","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show f22a806 --stat"},"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\n build.rs               |   8 ++++\n src/ast.rs             |   8 ++++\n src/check/linearity.rs |   4 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       |  20 +++++++---\n src/emit_go.rs         |   6 +++\n src/emit_jq.rs         |   4 ++\n src/emit_js.rs         |  23 +++++++++--\n src/emit_llvm.rs       |   6 +++\n src/emit_lua.rs        |   3 ++\n src/emit_py.rs         |   2 +\n src/emit_rs.rs         |   5 +++\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   1 +\n src/float.rs           |  16 ++++++++\n src/input.rs           |   6 +++\n src/lib.rs             |   1 +\n src/parse.rs           | 104 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------\n src/tags.rs            |   2 +\n src/tir.rs             |   5 +++\n src/ty.rs              |  14 ++++++-\n tests/backend_js.rs    |  44 +++++++++++++++++++++\n 20 files changed, 258 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"commit f22a8060efeb4cc81b8722763ee401b0943bca2e\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Wed Sep 2 10:08:40 2026 +0200\n\n    Add Float for the JS backend (IEEE binary64, ADR 0007)\n    \n    A decimal-point literal is a Float: a separate type from Int with no\n    implicit conversion, so mixing the two in one operator is an error the\n    same way mixing Int and Int64 is. Division is total -- a zero divisor\n    returns Infinity, the IEEE answer, where Int stops the program -- and\n    NaN and Infinity print as their names. A Float reads off the wire as\n    the JSON number it already is, the case that made Int64 refuse input.\n    JS emits plain number operators and prints through String().\n    \n    The other six backends get unreachable arms for Float nodes and types:\n    their exhaustive matches over the shared Type/Kind enums have to\n    compile, and Float never reaches them while it is JS-only. Their real\n    implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\n    \n    Derived: binary64 representation and total division (ADR 0007, the Q37\n    ruling), Float-in-input reading a JSON number, the separate-type and\n    no-implicit-conversion rules (Int64's precedent)\n    Agent-invented: float literal grammar (. and exponent only join when\n    digits follow), the shortest-round-trip literal spelling in src/float.rs\n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash <deepseek-v4-flash>\n\n build.rs               |   8 ++++\n src/ast.rs             |   8 ++++\n src/check/linearity.rs |   4 +-\n src/check/mod.rs       |  20 +++++++---\n src/emit_go.rs         |   6 +++\n src/emit_jq.rs         |   4 ++\n src/emit_js.rs         |  23 +++++++++--\n src/emit_llvm.rs       |   6 +++\n src/emit_lua.rs        |   3 ++\n src/emit_py.rs         |   2 +\n src/emit_rs.rs         |   5 +++\n src/emit_toylang.rs    |   1 +\n src/float.rs           |  16 ++++++++\n src/input.rs           |   6 +++\n src/lib.rs             |   1 +\n src/parse.rs           | 104 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------\n src/tags.rs            |   2 +\n src/tir.rs             |   5 +++\n src/ty.rs              |  14 ++++++-\n tests/backend_js.rs    |  44 +++++++++++++++++++++\n 20 files changed, 258 insertions(+), 24 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show f22a806 --stat","time":{"start":1788384558544,"end":1788384558546}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So the board rows for float-build-rust/python/go are \"delegated\" (stalled lanes per the escalation ruling — they were paused pending a float-format-research spike that hasn't been filed). And my lane float-build-lua is \"todo\" — I was dispatched now.\n\nNow the key question: the \"host hook\" term in the brief. The JS backend's Float support uses the host language's native double representation. For Lua, that's also native: Lua 5.4 has floats (math.type etc.), but the concern is formatting.\n\nLet me think carefully about the printing requirement. The issue says \"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a format and hold every backend to it.\"\n\nThe JS implementation picked: shortest round-trip decimal (float::lit uses Rust's f64 Display; JS String(number) produces the same; and printing uses String(value), giving \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\"/\"-Infinity\").\n\nFor Lua to agree byte-for-byte with JS on printing, we need a Lua-side formatting function that:\n1. Produces the shortest decimal that round-trips to the same double.\n\nBut wait — how do we do \"shortest round-trip\" in Lua? Lua's standard library doesn't have one. Options:\na. Use string.format(\"%.17g\", x) — 17 sig digits round-trips (always, per C standard minimum), but is NOT shortest and would disagree with JS on many values (e.g. JS String(0.1+0.2) = \"0.30000000000000004\"; %0.17g would give \"0.30000000000000004\" — wait, that IS 17 sig digits. Hmm interesting. Let me think: 0.1+0.2 = 0.30000000000000004 exactly (the double). The shortest round-trip repr is \"0.30000000000000004\" (17 digits). And %0.17g would produce \"0.30000000000000004\" too. But for 1.5, %0.17g gives \"1.5\" (17 sig digits strips trailing zeros? No — %g uses P decimals (precision P = significant digits)and strips trailing zeros. So %0.17g of 1.5 gives \"1.5\". And JS String(1.5) = \"1.5\". Agree.\n\nBut for 0.1, %0.17g gives \"0.1\"? %g with precision 17: 0.1 → 0.10000000000000001? Hmm. 0.1 as a double is 0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625. %g prints P significant digits. 17 sig digits of that: 0.10000000000000001. Trailing zeros stripped? %g doesn't strip significant digits — it uses %e format essentially with precision P-1. So 0.10000000000000000555... with 17 sig digits: \"0.10000000000000001\". That does NOT equal JS's String(0.1)=\"0.1\". Disagree!\n\nSo %0.17g disagrees with shortest round-trip on simple values. That's why the spike exists.\n\nHmm — so what does Lua's `tostring` do? For Lua 5.4, `tostring(0.1)` gives \"0.1\". Interesting — Lua uses `%.14g`, and for 0.1 the 14 sig digit repr is 0.10000000000000 → trailing zeros stripped → \"0.1\". And JS gives \"0.1\". Agree. But for 0.30000000000000004, Lua gives \"0.3\" and JS gives \"0.30000000000000004\". Disagree.\n\nSo neither Lua's tostring nor %0.17g matches JS shortest-round-trip for all values.\n\nThe float-format-research spike would settle how to make all backends agree on printing — but it hasn't been done yet. My brief explicitly says: \"Follow float-build's existing JS implementation as the reference pattern\" and \"mirror the same host-hook approach for Lua\". The host-hook approach for JS was: use the host's native shortest-round-trip formatting via String(value). For Lua, the host's native `tostring` is %.14g — not shortest-round-trip. Hmm.\n\nBut wait — maybe I should not overthink. Let me look at what the JS Float implementation actually does for printing more carefully: `show` for Type::Float uses `String({value})` — JS's shortest round-trip. And float::lit is used for literal spelling. Now the question: does the corpus actually compare Float output? The JS backend is the only one carrying Float so far, so no corpus float cases exist yet. When Lua carries Float, could we add corpus cases? No — the other five backends still refuse Float. So no cross-backend byte-agreement is exercised until more backends join.\n\n\n\nSo for THIS lane, what does \"mirror the same host-hook approach\" mean concretely?\n1. `Kind::Float(n)` → emit `crate::float::lit(*n)` — the literal spelling is the same for Lua (Lua number literals: a decimal point or exponent makes it a float literal in Lua 5.4; `1.5` is a float). And float::lit produces shortest round-trip, e.g. \"0.30000000000000004\" — valid Lua float literal.\n2. Arithmetic: plain Lua operators (+ - * /)). In Lua 5.4, division on floats → float; `1.0/0.0` → `inf`. And integers/floats: Lua's `/` always produces a float even for integers; `1/0` = inf (division by zero doesn't error — Lua floats/ints? In Lua 5.3+, integer division by zero: `1/0` where 1 is integer... Actually `/` on two integers converts them to floats and returns float. `1/0` = \"inf\". But that's for Int type — toylang Int division errors on zero (via tl_div guard).. Only Float type reaches plain `/`.\n3. Printing: `tostring(value)` for Float? That gives \"inf\" and \"nan\" not \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\". So we need a printing path that produces the same as JS's String: shortest round-trip + \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\"/\"-Infinity\". Lua's native tostring gives \"inf\"/\"nan\". Disagree. So we need either:\n   a. A host hook that catches inf/nan and spells them \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\"/\"-Infinity\", plus tostring otherwise (accepting that tostring's %.14g disagrees with JS on some round-trip values), OR\n   b. A full shortest-round-trip formatter in Lua (complex, and the exact algorithm is what the float-format-research spike would decide).\n\nThe issue's own acceptance criteria says: \"NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\" and\"corpus test coverage added for the above\". And Q37 (not yet settled: exact float literal printing. \n\nHmm. Wait — let me re-read the issue: \"Not yet settled, needs a call during implementation: exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages) -- pick a format and hold every backend to it.\"\n\n\"Float literal printing\" — printing of float LITERALS (the spelling of literals in emitted code?, or printing of float VALUES (output))? The phrase \"so all seven backends agree byte for byte\" — for the emitted code to be byte-identical? No. It's about output agreement. Actually re-reading: \"exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte (default float formatting differs across languages)\". Hmm, \"float literal printing\" — I think this is about how a float VALUE prints as output (the literal spelling of the value in output). The float::lit doc says \"How a Float is spelled as a number literal\" — that's the literal in the EMITTED source. And the printing of values (output) is handled separately per backend.\n\n\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. In toylang, the SAME program runs on all backends and prints results. There are two places floats appear as text:\n1. In emitted source: float literals. float::lit handles this — already settled (Rust f64 Display = shortest round-trip).\n2. In program output: printing a Float value. Each backend's printer. JS uses String(value).\n\nThe Q37 open question is about #2 (output printing), I believe — how do floats print in output so backends agree byte-for-byte. Actually the issue text says \"exact float literal printing\" but I think it means float value printing/formatting generally.\n\nRegardless, for THIS lane, I need to make a call for the Lua backend that mirrors JS. The JS choice: shortest round-trip decimal via host's native formatting. For Lua, the closest host-native equivalent is `tostring`. But `tostring` differs from shortest-round-trip in two ways:\n   - inf/nan → \"inf\"/\"-inf\"/\"nan\"/\"-nan\" vs \"Infinity\"/\"-Infinity\"/\"NaN\".\n   - %.14g vs shortest round-trip for some values.\n\nHmm. Wait — is there a shorter way? Lua 5.4's string.format supports %g with arbitrary precision. And there's a trick: format with %.17g then strip? Lua doesn't have a shortest-round-trip primitive. So we'd need to implement Ryū/Grisu in Lua — not a \"host hook\". That's clearly NOT what this lane wants (\"host hook\" — use the host's native machinery).\n\nLet me reconsider what the lane title means by \"host hook\". In the JS backend, was there an actual \"host hook\"? Let me grep the JS emit for how Float values reach the \"host\". Hmm — maybe \"host hook\" means that the Float type is natively supported by the host language (as opposed to being emulated), and the backend just hands the float to the host's native operators/formatting. I think that's it. \"host-hooks\" = uses host's native IEEE double.\n\n\n\nNow, the concrete printing decision. Let me look at what tests exist and what the JS implementation committed. Let me look at commit f22a806 \"Add Float for the JS backend\".","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06406bdb0001jwHMw5mM8vcCB1","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_064067ba30010gO5lT1vXBVnfY"}}
