{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788384563745,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_06406d21f001T90H5UWX8wBg4h","messageID":"msg_06406bde6001atZbp9DCwtB5US","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","snapshot":"af4fa15ed4996d0bb919f8e70bd0271c9d7c1cdd","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788384570404,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_06406ea2f001I4ggMJxNZrLmx2","messageID":"msg_06406bde6001atZbp9DCwtB5US","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","type":"text","text":"Let me verify Lua 5.4's float formatting behavior empirically:\n\n","time":{"start":1788384569904,"end":1788384570398}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788384570406,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_b51fe0df70174a27b58e317f","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"lua5.4 -e 'print(tostring(0.1))); print(tostring(0.1+0.2))); print(tostring(1.5))); print(tostring(1/0))); print(tostring(0/0))); print(tostring(-1/0)))'"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788384570395,"end":1788384570404}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The JS commit is the reference. Note \"Their real implementations are follow-up rows, not this one.\" — I'm doing the Lua follow-up row now.\n\nNow, my decision on printing for Lua. The JS reference uses `String(value)` which gives:\n- shortest round-trip decimal for finite values\n- \"Infinity\", \"-Infinity\", \"NaN\" for non-finite.\n\n\n\nFor Lua, what is the closest host-native approach that agrees byte-for-byte with JS on the tests that exist? Let me check: Lua `tostring` gives \"inf\", \"nan\", etc. — NOT matching. So the \"host hook\" for printing cannot be bare `tostring`.\n\nOptions for printing a Float in Lua:\n aggree with JS's \"Infinity\"/\"NaN\"/\"-Infinity\" names and shortest-round-trip:\n\n1. Emit a helper `tl_float_str(x)`:\n   - if x != x then return \"NaN\"\n   - if x == math.huge then return \"Infinity\"\n   - if x == -math.huge then return \"-Infinity\"\n   - else return tostring(x)  (or a better formatter)\n2. Use string.format with %g and hand-roll... \n\nThe short answer: the tests that would be added here are the same five tests as backend_js.rs's float tests (or extended to Lua+JS agreement).. The tests expect \"2\", \"Infinity\", \"NaN\", \"-Infinity\", \"true\", \"5\" — all round numbers and non-finite names. For those, Lua's tostring produces \"2\", \"inf\", \"nan\", \"-inf\", \"5\" — the finite ones agree (\"2\", \"5\"), the non-finite names disagree.\n\n So at minimum, a helper is needed to map inf/nan to the JS names.\n\nNow the deeper question: do we need shortest-round-trip for finite values in Lua? For the existing tests, tostring is fine. For future corpus agreement, we'd need to agree with JS byte-for-byte — but that's exactly the unresolved Q37/spike. Since the brief says mirror the JS approach (shortest round-trip via host native), and Lua's host native (tostring) does NOT do shortest round-trip, I have a genuine choice:\n\nOption A: Use tostring for finite, + name mapping for non-finite. Simple, host-native, but disagrees with JS on values needing >14 sig digits (e.g. 0.30000000000000004 → Lua \"0.3\" vs JS \"0.30000000000000004\").\n\nOption B: Implement shortest-round-trip formatting in Lua (complex, pre-empts the spike).\n\nOption C: Use string.format(\"%.17g\")... doesn't give shortest either, and disagrees on simple values (0.1 → \"0.10000000000000001\"). Worse.\n\n Option D: Emit a helper that finds the shortest round-trip by testing candidate precisions: for p in 1..17, format with %.{p}g, parse it back, if it round-trips (== value then use it. This is a simple host-native-ish algorithm that finds the shortest decimal that round-trips! Lua can parse with tonumber. This would actually match JS's shortest-round-trip output byte-for-byte (given both use the same shortest-round-trip selection rule and %.g formatting matches JS's dtoa formatting conventions? Mostly — %g formatting differs from shortest-round-trip in details like exponent formats (\"1e+21\" vs \"1e+21\"in JS...). Hmm.\n\nWait. Let me actually check: is Lua 5.4's tostring REALLY %.14g? Let me test empirically with lua5.4 (available at /usr/bin/lua5.4).. Note mlua vendors Lua 5.4 too — same version, hopefully same behavior. Let me run some experiments.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06406ec100019vJrG2EG4SFkq4","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","messageID":"msg_06406bde6001atZbp9DCwtB5US"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788384570413,"sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","part":{"id":"prt_06406ec2b001J6se6jocHBTA78","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"af4fa15ed4996d0bb919f8e70bd0271c9d7c1cdd","messageID":"msg_06406bde6001atZbp9DCwtB5US","sessionID":"ses_f9bfb8556ffen7Iyy24hTmtXJA","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":81431,"input":2905,"output":163,"reasoning":795,"cache":{"write":0,"read":77568}},"cost":0.001602353}}
